// AAMC Question Bank Flashcards: original recall questions written from the concepts tested in the
// AAMC Biology Question Pack, Vol. 1. No AAMC passages, questions or explanations are reproduced.
// Format: {s: tag, disc: scoring discipline, aamc: content category, q, choices: [4], ans: 0-3, exp, src}
const QB_AAMC_BIO = [
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2A',q:'A red blood cell is placed in distilled water. What happens to it?',choices:['Water leaves by osmosis and the cell shrinks','Water enters by osmosis and the cell swells and may lyse','Solute diffuses out and cell volume stays constant','Water enters by active transport and the cell swells'],ans:1,exp:'Distilled water is **hypotonic** to the cytoplasm, so water moves by osmosis toward the higher solute concentration inside the cell; the RBC swells and can burst (**hemolysis**). Shrinking (crenation) happens only in a hypertonic solution, where water flows out.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-P001'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2A',q:'An animal cell is placed in a hypertonic solution. What happens to it?',choices:['It swells and may lyse','It shows no net volume change','It shrinks (crenation)','It swells, then returns to normal size'],ans:2,exp:'A **hypertonic** solution has more solute than the cytoplasm, so water leaves the cell by osmosis and it shrinks (**crenation**). Swelling and lysis occur in hypotonic solutions, and no net change occurs in isotonic ones.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-P002'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2A',q:'Why does severe hyperglycemia dehydrate body cells?',choices:['Glucose enters cells and drags water out with it','Insulin opens aquaporins that export water from cells','Glucose blocks ADH release, so cells lose water to urine','Glucose raises extracellular osmolarity, drawing water out of cells'],ans:3,exp:'Excess glucose raises the **osmolarity** of the extracellular fluid, so water moves out of cells by osmosis. Osmotic diuresis adds urinary water loss, but the direct cause of cellular dehydration is the osmotic gradient, not a change in ADH.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-P003'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'Which hormone lowers blood glucose, and which pancreatic cells secrete it?',choices:['Glucagon / alpha cells','Insulin / beta cells','Insulin / alpha cells','Glucagon / beta cells'],ans:1,exp:'**Insulin** from pancreatic **beta cells** promotes glucose uptake and storage, lowering blood glucose. Glucagon comes from alpha cells and raises glucose, as do epinephrine and cortisol.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-P004'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'Which finding best suggests that normal blood glucose regulation has been overridden?',choices:['Low glucose and low insulin after an overnight fast','Rising insulin shortly after a carbohydrate-rich meal','High glucose and high insulin with ongoing liver glycogenolysis','Liver glycogenolysis while blood glucose is falling'],ans:2,exp:'High glucose normally triggers insulin, which **stops hepatic glycogenolysis**; if the liver keeps releasing glucose despite high glucose and high insulin, the feedback loop is being overridden (e.g., by stress hormones or insulin resistance). Glycogenolysis while glucose is falling is the expected glucagon response.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-P005'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2A',q:'In freeze-tolerant animals, where does ice normally form?',choices:['Only inside cells, within the cytosol','Equally inside and outside cells','Only within organelles such as vacuoles','Only outside cells, in plasma and interstitial fluid'],ans:3,exp:'Freeze-tolerant animals confine ice to **extracellular** compartments (plasma, lymph, interstitial spaces). Intracellular ice crystals rupture membranes and organelles and are lethal, so ice inside cells is never tolerated.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-P006'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2A',q:'How do intracellular cryoprotectants such as glucose protect cells as ice forms outside them?',choices:['They seed small ice crystals inside cells at a controlled rate','They raise cytosolic solute, lowering freezing point and limiting water loss','They make the plasma membrane impermeable to water','They actively pump water out of cells before freezing begins'],ans:1,exp:'As extracellular ice forms, the remaining extracellular fluid becomes concentrated and pulls water out of cells. High intracellular solute (**colligative** freezing-point depression) keeps the cytosol liquid and reduces this osmotic water loss; seeding ice inside cells would be lethal.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-P007'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'Two strains of the same bacterial species differ in virulence. What usually explains the difference?',choices:['Differences in their ribosomal RNA sequences','Different numbers of chromosomes in each strain','Virulence genes gained by horizontal transfer, often on plasmids','Differences in histone modifications between strains'],ans:2,exp:'Strain differences usually come from **virulence factors** carried on plasmids or genomic islands acquired by transformation, transduction or conjugation. rRNA is highly conserved within a species (it is used to define species), and bacteria do not regulate genes through eukaryotic-style histone modifications.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-P008'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'Why does not every mutation or chronic infection lead to cancer?',choices:['Mutations in somatic cells are always repaired before division','Cancer needs multiple hits, and most damaged cells are cleared','Somatic mutations cannot be passed on to daughter cells','Oncogenes are recessive, so both copies must be mutated first'],ans:1,exp:'Carcinogenesis requires **multiple hits**: activation of oncogenes plus loss of tumor suppressors, while DNA repair, apoptosis (e.g., via p53) and immune surveillance eliminate most precancerous cells. Oncogenes act dominantly, so one activated copy is enough; it is tumor suppressors that usually need both alleles lost.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-P009'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2A',q:'Per ATP hydrolyzed, how many ions does the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase move, and in which directions?',choices:['2 Na⁺ out / 3 K⁺ in','3 Na⁺ in / 2 K⁺ out','2 Na⁺ in / 3 K⁺ out','3 Na⁺ out / 2 K⁺ in'],ans:3,exp:'Each cycle uses 1 ATP to pump **3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in**, exporting one net positive charge, so the pump is **electrogenic** and contributes slightly to the negative interior. Swapping the two numbers is the most common error.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-P010'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2A',q:'Which tissue relies most heavily on the Na⁺/K⁺ pump to power bulk transport?',choices:['Adipose tissue','Hyaline cartilage','Renal tubule cells','Mature red blood cells'],ans:2,exp:'Renal tubule cells reabsorb most filtered Na⁺, and their basolateral **Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase** creates the gradient that drives secondary active transport of glucose, amino acids and more; intestinal epithelium and neurons are also heavy users. Red blood cells have pumps but low transport demand.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-P011'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'What directly causes the depolarization and repolarization of a neuronal action potential?',choices:['Voltage-gated Na⁺ and K⁺ channels opening','The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase reversing direction','Ligand-gated Cl⁻ channels opening','The Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger reversing direction'],ans:0,exp:'Rapid Na⁺ influx through **voltage-gated Na⁺ channels** depolarizes the membrane, and K⁺ efflux through voltage-gated K⁺ channels repolarizes it. The Na⁺/K⁺ pump does not generate the spike; it slowly maintains the gradients the channels depend on.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-P012'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'What is the net yield of glycolysis per molecule of glucose?',choices:['4 ATP and 2 NADH','2 ATP and 2 FADH₂','2 ATP and 2 NADH','2 ATP and 4 NADH'],ans:2,exp:'Glycolysis makes 4 ATP by substrate-level phosphorylation but spends 2 (at hexokinase and PFK-1), for **2 net ATP**, plus **2 NADH** from glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase. Four ATP is the gross yield, and FADH₂ comes from the citric acid cycle, not glycolysis.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-P013'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'When circulation stops and O₂ runs out, why do cells convert pyruvate to lactate?',choices:['To produce additional ATP from lactate','To regenerate FAD for the citric acid cycle','To feed lactate into the electron transport chain','To regenerate NAD⁺ so glycolysis can continue'],ans:3,exp:'Without O₂ as the final electron acceptor, the electron transport chain stops and NADH accumulates; **lactate dehydrogenase** reoxidizes NADH to **NAD⁺** so glycolysis can keep running. The lactate step itself makes no ATP.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-P014'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'What mainly determines how much water the body loses through the skin?',choices:['Glomerular filtration rate','Body temperature driving sweat','Dietary salt intake','Daily fluid intake'],ans:1,exp:'When the **hypothalamus** senses heat from the environment or exercise, it increases sweat secretion, and evaporation cools the skin; this is the main variable in skin water loss. Kidney function, salt and water intake regulate urinary water loss, not loss through the skin.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-001'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which is an example of insensible water loss?',choices:['Sweat during exercise','Urine from the kidneys','Vapor in exhaled breath','Water in diarrheal stool'],ans:2,exp:'**Insensible** losses are passive and unnoticed: water diffusing through the skin and leaving as vapor in exhaled air. Sweat and urine are **sensible** (measurable) losses; heat raises skin water loss mainly by increasing sweat.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-001b'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'Lipases hydrolyze many different esters rather than one specific molecule. What explains this broad specificity?',choices:['They have multiple active sites, one for each substrate','They act only after bile fully emulsifies the substrate','They recognize the shared ester bond, not one exact molecule','They bind substrates covalently through a disulfide bond'],ans:2,exp:'Enzyme **specificity** describes how narrow a range of substrates an enzyme acts on. Lipases recognize the common **ester linkage** of fats and other carboxylic acid esters, so they act on many molecules; bile emulsification increases surface area but does not determine which bonds lipase can cleave.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-002'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'Which enzyme is the classic example of absolute specificity, acting on only one substrate?',choices:['Lipase','Hexokinase','Trypsin','Urease'],ans:3,exp:'**Urease** hydrolyzes only urea, the textbook case of **absolute specificity**. Hexokinase phosphorylates several hexoses, and lipases and peptidases act on one type of bond across many different molecules.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-002b'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'Which bacterial gene-transfer process requires the F (fertility) plasmid?',choices:['Transformation','Conjugation','Transduction','Transposition'],ans:1,exp:'In **conjugation**, an F⁺ cell uses genes on the F plasmid to build a **sex pilus**, contacts an F⁻ cell and transfers a copy of the plasmid. Transformation (uptake of free DNA) and transduction (phage-carried DNA) need no F plasmid.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-003'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'In which process is bacterial DNA carried into a recipient cell by a bacteriophage?',choices:['Transduction','Transformation','Conjugation','Binary fission'],ans:0,exp:'In **transduction**, a phage accidentally packages host DNA and injects it into the next bacterium it infects. Transformation is uptake of free environmental DNA, and conjugation is direct transfer through a sex pilus.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-003b'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'What distinguishes an Hfr cell from an ordinary F⁺ cell?',choices:['Its F plasmid has been lost entirely','It carries two copies of the F plasmid','It lacks the genes needed to build a pilus','Its F plasmid is integrated into the chromosome'],ans:3,exp:'In an **Hfr** (high frequency of recombination) cell, the F plasmid has integrated into the bacterial chromosome, so during conjugation it can transfer chromosomal genes along with part of the F sequence. A cell that has lost the F plasmid is simply F⁻.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-003c'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'A cross produces some offspring showing both recessive traits (aabb) and some that do not. What must be true of the parents?',choices:['Both parents are AaBb','At least one parent is AA or BB','Each parent carries at least one a and one b allele','Both parents are aa for one of the genes'],ans:2,exp:'An aabb offspring needs an **a and a b allele from each parent**, so every parent must carry both recessive alleles. AaBb × AaBb is the classic case but not the only one (Aabb × aaBb also works); an AA or BB parent could never produce aabb offspring.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-004'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'In a dihybrid cross AaBb × AaBb with independent assortment, what fraction of offspring are aabb?',choices:['1/16','1/4','3/16','9/16'],ans:0,exp:'Each gene independently gives 1/4 homozygous recessive, so aabb = 1/4 × 1/4 = **1/16**, the "1" of the **9:3:3:1** ratio. The 3/16 classes show one dominant and one recessive trait.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-004b'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'In a test cross, an organism with a dominant phenotype is crossed with a homozygous recessive. What does the appearance of any recessive offspring show?',choices:['The tested parent is homozygous dominant','The two genes are linked on one chromosome','The trait shows incomplete dominance','The tested parent is heterozygous'],ans:3,exp:'The homozygous recessive partner can give only a recessive allele, so a recessive offspring must also have received a **recessive allele from the tested parent**, making it heterozygous. A homozygous dominant parent would produce only dominant-phenotype offspring.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-004c'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'How do mammals inflate their lungs during inspiration?',choices:['Mouth and throat muscles push air into the lungs under pressure','O₂ diffuses down its partial pressure gradient to fill the alveoli','Thoracic expansion drops alveolar pressure below atmospheric','Smooth muscle in the alveolar walls contracts to expand them'],ans:2,exp:'Contraction of the **diaphragm** and external intercostals enlarges the thoracic cavity, so intrapleural and then alveolar pressure fall below atmospheric and air flows in (**negative-pressure breathing**). Diffusion handles gas exchange, not inflation, and pushing air in (buccal pumping) is what frogs do.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-005'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'During quiet breathing at rest, which phase is passive and what drives it?',choices:['Exhalation / elastic recoil of the lungs','Inhalation / diaphragm relaxation','Exhalation / internal intercostal contraction','Inhalation / elastic recoil of the chest wall'],ans:0,exp:'Quiet inhalation is active (diaphragm and external intercostals contract), while quiet exhalation is passive: the muscles relax and the lungs **recoil elastically**. Internal intercostals and abdominal muscles are recruited only for forced exhalation.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-005b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Why does a puncture in the chest wall (pneumothorax) cause the lung to collapse?',choices:['Intrapleural pressure falls further, pulling the lung inward','Intrapleural pressure rises to atmospheric, so the lung recoils','Surfactant leaks out, raising alveolar surface tension','The diaphragm is paralyzed and stops contracting'],ans:1,exp:'Normally the **negative intrapleural pressure** holds the lung against the chest wall. When air enters the pleural space, that pressure equalizes with the atmosphere, nothing opposes the lung\'s elastic recoil, and it collapses.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-005c'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which organism inflates its lungs by positive-pressure (buccal pumping) breathing?',choices:['Human','Dog','Bat','Frog'],ans:3,exp:'Amphibians such as **frogs** fill the mouth with air and then raise the floor of the mouth to push it into the lungs (**positive pressure**). Mammals, including bats, use negative-pressure breathing; mechanical ventilators are the main positive-pressure exception in humans.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-005d'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'For extrachromosomal genes to be inherited by daughter cells, what must the DNA carrying them have?',choices:['A centromere for spindle attachment','Telomeres at both ends','Its own origin of replication','A histone-free circular structure'],ans:2,exp:'Extrachromosomal DNA such as **plasmids**, mitochondrial DNA and chloroplast DNA must replicate independently, which requires its **own origin of replication**, so copies reach each daughter cell. Plasmids and mtDNA lack centromeres and telomeres, yet are still inherited.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-006'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'Which enzyme transcribes most rRNA genes in eukaryotes, and where?',choices:['RNA polymerase II / nucleoplasm','RNA polymerase I / nucleolus','RNA polymerase III / cytoplasm','RNA polymerase I / cytoplasm'],ans:1,exp:'**RNA polymerase I** transcribes the large rRNA precursor in the **nucleolus**; cells carry many repeated rRNA gene copies because they need huge numbers of ribosomes. RNA polymerase II makes mRNA and RNA polymerase III makes tRNA and 5S rRNA, all within the nucleus.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-006b'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'What is a cell with more than two complete sets of chromosomes called?',choices:['Haploid','Diploid','Aneuploid','Polyploid'],ans:3,exp:'**Polyploid** cells have more than two full sets (3n, 4n or more), giving many copies of each gene and potentially higher expression. Aneuploidy is the gain or loss of individual chromosomes, not whole sets.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-006c'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'How does germ-line DNA differ from somatic DNA?',choices:['Germ-line DNA is passed to offspring; somatic DNA is not inherited','Somatic DNA is passed to offspring; germ-line DNA is not inherited','Germ-line DNA is used for expression; somatic DNA stays silent','Germ-line DNA lacks introns; somatic DNA still contains them'],ans:0,exp:'**Germ-line** DNA goes through meiosis into gametes and must stay intact, whereas **somatic** DNA serves gene expression in the current organism and is not inherited. Ciliates make this split physical, keeping a germ-line micronucleus and a separate expression macronucleus.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-006d'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'Which checkpoint confirms that DNA has been completely and correctly replicated before mitosis begins?',choices:['G1/S checkpoint','Spindle (metaphase) checkpoint','G2/M checkpoint','Cytokinesis checkpoint'],ans:2,exp:'DNA is replicated in **S phase**, and the **G2/M checkpoint** verifies complete, undamaged replication before the cell enters mitosis. The G1/S checkpoint decides whether to replicate at all, and the spindle checkpoint checks kinetochore attachment.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-006e'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'What distinguishes prokaryotic binary fission from eukaryotic mitosis?',choices:['Binary fission produces four genetically distinct cells','Binary fission requires the nuclear envelope to break down','Binary fission occurs only after crossing over','Binary fission has no spindle separating condensed chromosomes'],ans:3,exp:'In **binary fission**, prokaryotes replicate a circular chromosome and split in two without the spindle-driven separation of condensed chromosomes seen in **mitosis**. Prokaryotes have no nuclear envelope to break down, and both processes yield two identical cells.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-006f'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'What is the main purpose of mitosis compared with amitotic nuclear division?',choices:['Producing daughter cells with half the chromosome number','Ensuring each daughter gets an identical, complete chromosome set','Generating genetic variation through crossing over','Increasing the DNA content of each daughter nucleus'],ans:1,exp:'Mitosis lines up chromosomes at the metaphase plate and separates **sister chromatids** so each daughter receives an identical, complete set. In **amitosis**, DNA is split roughly and unevenly, so daughters may get too much or too little; halving chromosome number is the job of meiosis.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-007'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'A passage reports that a cell adds an extra S phase only when its nucleus is small. What is the most plausible trigger?',choices:['A signal from mitochondria reporting high ATP','A receptor sensing large overall cell volume','A sensor detecting low DNA content in that nucleus','A sensor detecting excess DNA in a neighboring cell'],ans:2,exp:'A compensating response is best explained by a sensor of the very **deficiency it corrects**: low DNA content in the same nucleus, which an extra S phase restores. Answers that tie the response to unrelated structures or the wrong compartment, such as mitochondrial ATP, do not explain why only small nuclei respond.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-007b'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'What is the centromere?',choices:['The DNA region joining sister chromatids, where kinetochores attach','The microtubule-organizing center that builds the spindle','The pair of barrel-shaped structures within the centrosome','The protein ring that pinches the cell during cytokinesis'],ans:0,exp:'The **centromere** is a DNA region that holds sister chromatids together and serves as the assembly site for the **kinetochore**, which binds spindle microtubules. The similarly named centrosome is the microtubule-organizing center, containing centrioles in animal cells.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-007c'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'Which molecules drive the cell forward through the phases of the cell cycle?',choices:['p53 and Rb proteins','Cyclins and cyclin-dependent kinases','Caspases and cytochrome c','Histones and DNA methyltransferases'],ans:1,exp:'**Cyclins** rise and fall during the cycle and activate **CDKs**, which phosphorylate targets that push each phase forward. p53 and Rb act as brakes at checkpoints, halting progression when DNA damage or other conditions are not met.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-007d'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'In oogenesis, _____ is divided unequally between daughter cells, while _____ is divided equally.',choices:['chromosomes / cytoplasm','chromosomes / organelles','cytoplasm / chromosomes','cytoplasm / mitochondria'],ans:2,exp:'Unequal **cytokinesis** leaves almost all the cytoplasm in the ovum and very little in polar bodies, but **chromosomes** are segregated equally, as in any meiosis. Uneven chromosome division would produce aneuploid gametes.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-008'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'What is the main function of polar bodies in oogenesis?',choices:['Supplying nutrients to the ovum after fertilization','Producing hormones that support the corpus luteum','Serving as backup ova if the first is not fertilized','Discarding extra chromosome sets while keeping cytoplasm in one egg'],ans:3,exp:'**Polar bodies** receive a full chromosome set but little cytoplasm, letting the oocyte halve its chromosome number while concentrating nutrients, organelles and mRNAs in one large egg. Each primary oocyte yields 1 ovum plus 2–3 polar bodies, which degenerate rather than nourish or replace the egg.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-008b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'At ovulation, the human secondary oocyte is arrested at _____, and it completes meiosis II only after _____.',choices:['prophase I / puberty','metaphase II / fertilization','metaphase II / the LH surge','prophase II / implantation'],ans:1,exp:'Primary oocytes arrest in **prophase I** from fetal life until shortly before their own ovulation, when the LH surge lets them finish meiosis I. The secondary oocyte then arrests in **metaphase II** and completes meiosis II only if a sperm fertilizes it.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-008c'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'How many functional gametes come from one primary spermatocyte versus one primary oocyte?',choices:['4 sperm / 4 ova','2 sperm / 1 ovum','2 sperm / 2 ova','4 sperm / 1 ovum'],ans:3,exp:'Spermatogenesis divides cytoplasm equally, giving **4 sperm** per primary spermatocyte, while unequal cytokinesis in oogenesis gives **1 ovum** plus polar bodies per primary oocyte.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-008d'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'A polyploid nucleus carrying two alleles of a gene divides repeatedly by random, uneven splitting instead of mitosis. What happens to the alleles over many divisions?',choices:['Their ratio stays exactly 1:1 in every daughter nucleus','Both alleles are quickly lost from all lineages','Their ratio drifts, and some lineages keep only one allele','The alleles recombine to form new hybrid alleles'],ans:2,exp:'Random, uneven partitioning makes allele ratios **drift** from cell to cell, and repeated divisions can leave some lineages with only one allele (**assortment**). Losing every copy of a chromosome is unlikely because a polyploid nucleus carries so many copies.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-009'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'When does crossing over between homologous chromosomes occur?',choices:['Prophase I of meiosis','Metaphase I of meiosis','Prophase II of meiosis','Anaphase of mitosis'],ans:0,exp:'Homologs pair through the **synaptonemal complex** in **prophase I** and exchange segments at chiasmata. Metaphase I is when homolog pairs line up for independent assortment, and crossing over is not a normal feature of mitosis.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-009b'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'Independent assortment of homologous chromosomes, a source of genetic variation, arises at which stage of meiosis?',choices:['Prophase I','Metaphase I','Metaphase II','Anaphase II'],ans:1,exp:'In **metaphase I**, each homolog pair orients randomly at the plate, so maternal and paternal chromosomes are sorted independently (**independent assortment**). Crossing over (prophase I), random fertilization and mutation are the other sources of variation.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-009c'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'A nucleus that only carries out day-to-day gene expression discards part of its DNA. Which genes could it lose without harm?',choices:['Genes encoding ribosomal RNA','Genes encoding RNA polymerase subunits','Genes encoding tRNAs','Genes required only for meiosis'],ans:3,exp:'A nucleus devoted to expression never undergoes meiosis, so **meiosis-specific genes** are dispensable. It must keep **housekeeping genes**, such as those for rRNA, tRNA and RNA polymerase, because transcription and translation are needed in every active cell.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-010'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'Which of the following is a housekeeping gene?',choices:['Insulin','Albumin','β-actin','β-globin'],ans:2,exp:'**Housekeeping genes** are expressed in essentially all cells for basic survival, for example cytoskeletal **actin**, RNA polymerase, ribosomal proteins and glycolytic enzymes. Insulin (beta cells), albumin (hepatocytes) and β-globin (erythroid precursors) are tissue-specific.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-010b'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'In a eukaryote, transcription occurs in the _____ and translation occurs in the _____.',choices:['nucleus / nucleus','cytoplasm / nucleus','nucleus / cytoplasm','cytoplasm / cytoplasm'],ans:2,exp:'Replication, transcription and RNA processing (**5′ cap**, poly-A tail, splicing) occur in the **nucleus**, and mature mRNA is exported for translation on ribosomes in the **cytoplasm**, free or on rough ER. This separation lets eukaryotes process RNA first, unlike prokaryotes, which couple the two steps.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-010c'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'An organism has a germ-line nucleus and a separate expression nucleus. Which determines the genotype its offspring inherit?',choices:['Only the expression nucleus','Only the germ-line nucleus','Both nuclei contribute equally','Whichever nucleus has more copies'],ans:1,exp:'Only the **germ-line nucleus** undergoes meiosis and is transmitted, so standard Mendelian ratios apply to its genotype (Aa × Aa gives 1/4 aa). The expression nucleus is tempting because it determines the parent\'s own phenotype, but it is not inherited.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-011'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'With complete dominance, what are the genotype and phenotype ratios from a monohybrid cross Aa × Aa?',choices:['3 AA : 1 aa / 3 : 1','1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa / 1 : 2 : 1','1 Aa : 1 aa / 1 : 1','1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa / 3 : 1'],ans:3,exp:'Each parent gives A or a with equal probability, so offspring are **1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa**; with complete dominance AA and Aa look alike, giving **3 dominant : 1 recessive** (25% recessive). A 1:2:1 phenotype ratio appears only with incomplete dominance or codominance.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-011b'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'For an autosomal trait with complete dominance, which genotype is required for the recessive phenotype to appear?',choices:['Homozygous recessive','Heterozygous','Homozygous dominant','Either homozygous genotype'],ans:0,exp:'**Genotype** is the set of alleles present and **phenotype** is the observable trait. A recessive trait shows only when both alleles are recessive (homozygous, aa); in a heterozygote (Aa) the dominant allele masks it.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-011c'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'An antisense oligonucleotide base-pairs with a complementary target mRNA. Which step of gene expression does it block?',choices:['Transcription','Translation','DNA replication','DNA repair'],ans:1,exp:'Pairing with the sense mRNA forms double-stranded RNA that ribosomes cannot read and that may be degraded, so the antisense molecule blocks **translation**. Because it acts on mRNA that already exists, it does not stop **transcription** of the gene.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-012'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'Which DNA strand has the same sequence as the mRNA, apart from T in place of U?',choices:['Template strand','Lagging strand','Leading strand','Coding strand'],ans:3,exp:'The **coding (sense) strand** matches the mRNA sequence except for T in place of U. RNA polymerase reads the complementary **template (antisense) strand**, which is why antisense drugs resemble the template-like complement of the mRNA.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-012b'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1B',q:'Which modification makes therapeutic oligonucleotides resistant to nuclease degradation?',choices:['Uracil in place of thymine','Ribose in place of deoxyribose','Phosphorothioate backbone','Shorter overall length'],ans:2,exp:'Nucleases hydrolyze the **phosphodiester** backbone; replacing a non-bridging oxygen with sulfur (**phosphorothioate**) resists this cleavage and extends the drug\'s half-life. Swapping in ribose makes the backbone more labile, since the 2′-OH promotes hydrolysis.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-012c'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'For an inserted antisense gene to block its target gene effectively, what matters most?',choices:['Insertion on the same chromosome as the target gene','Expression in the same cells and at the same times as the target','Insertion on the opposite DNA strand from the target','Placement immediately downstream of the target gene'],ans:1,exp:'Antisense RNA works only if it is present wherever and whenever the **sense mRNA** is made, so it needs **similar regulation** (matching promoter and enhancers). Its chromosomal location and strand do not matter, because the RNA acts in trans on the mRNA.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-013'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'Which statement about eukaryotic enhancers is accurate?',choices:['They can act from far away and in either orientation','They must lie immediately upstream of the TATA box','They bind RNA polymerase II directly to start transcription','They function only when located inside an intron'],ans:0,exp:'**Enhancers** bind activators and can boost transcription from thousands of base pairs away, upstream or downstream and in either orientation, by DNA looping to the promoter. RNA polymerase II binds the **promoter** (TATA box) with general transcription factors; chromatin state (histone acetylation opens it, DNA methylation silences it) also controls access.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-013b'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Can silencing a gene with antisense therapy treat a loss-of-function disease such as PKU?',choices:['Yes, because silencing restores normal enzyme activity','No, because antisense RNA cannot enter liver cells','Yes, because it lowers phenylalanine production','No, because the gene product is already missing or inactive'],ans:3,exp:'In **loss-of-function** disease such as PKU (deficient phenylalanine hydroxylase), the product is already absent or inactive, so silencing the gene changes nothing; treatment requires a working gene or enzyme, or restricting dietary phenylalanine. Antisense therapy helps when a gene product is **harmful or overexpressed**.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-014'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'Phenylketonuria (PKU) results from a deficiency of which enzyme?',choices:['Tyrosine hydroxylase','Phenylalanine hydroxylase','Homogentisate oxidase','Branched-chain α-keto acid dehydrogenase'],ans:1,exp:'PKU is an autosomal recessive loss of **phenylalanine hydroxylase**, which converts Phe to Tyr; Phe builds up and damages the brain, so patients follow a low-Phe diet (avoiding aspartame) and tyrosine becomes essential. Tyrosine hydroxylase acts one step later (Tyr → L-DOPA) in catecholamine synthesis.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-014b'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Loss-of-function mutations tend to be inherited as _____ traits, while gain-of-function mutations tend to be _____',choices:['dominant / recessive','recessive / recessive','dominant / dominant','recessive / dominant'],ans:3,exp:'One working copy usually makes enough product, so **loss-of-function** alleles are typically recessive; a **gain-of-function** allele adds new or excess activity that shows even beside a normal allele, so it is typically dominant. Exceptions exist (haploinsufficiency, dominant-negative alleles), but these are the default tendencies.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-014c'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'What is the RNA strand complementary to 5\'-CGAUAC-3\', written 5\'→3\'?',choices:['5\'-GCUAUG-3\'','5\'-GTATCG-3\'','5\'-GUAUCG-3\'','5\'-CAUAGC-3\''],ans:2,exp:'Pairing each base (C–G, G–C, A–U, U–A) gives 3\'-GCUAUG-5\', which rewritten 5\'→3\' is **5\'-GUAUCG-3\'**, because paired strands are antiparallel. 5\'-GCUAUG-3\' has the right bases in the wrong orientation, and any sequence containing T is DNA, not RNA.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-015'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'A student writes the RNA complement of 5\'-AUGC-3\' as 5\'-UACG-3\'. What error did the student make?',choices:['Ignored antiparallel orientation','Used thymine in place of uracil','Paired purines with purines','Swapped G–C pairs for A–U pairs'],ans:0,exp:'Each base is paired correctly, but the result is really 3\'-UACG-5\' and must be rewritten as **5\'-GCAU-3\'**; labeling it 5\'→3\' without reversing ignores that paired strands are **antiparallel**. No T appears, so the DNA-versus-RNA trap is not the error here.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-015b'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'To place a transgene in nearly every cell of an animal, which delivery strategy works best?',choices:['Retroviral vector into adult liver','Episomal plasmid into the early embryo','Adenoviral vector into adult blood','Retroviral vector into the early embryo'],ans:3,exp:'An **integrating** vector such as a retrovirus inserts the gene into a host chromosome, and doing so in the **early embryo** means every descendant cell inherits it. A non-integrating episomal plasmid is diluted out as cells divide, and adult delivery reaches only some tissues.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-016'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'Why can\'t a DNA- or RNA-based drug simply be swallowed as a pill?',choices:['It is too hydrophobic to dissolve in gut fluid','Pepsin cleaves its phosphodiester bonds','Nucleases degrade it, and it crosses membranes poorly','Its small size causes rapid renal excretion'],ans:2,exp:'Digestive **nucleases** and stomach acid break nucleic acids down, and their large, negatively charged backbone keeps them from crossing cell membranes. Pepsin is a protease that cleaves peptide bonds, not phosphodiester bonds.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-016b'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'Which enzyme inserts a retrovirus\'s DNA copy into the host chromosome?',choices:['Reverse transcriptase','Integrase','Transposase','DNA ligase'],ans:1,exp:'**Reverse transcriptase** first copies the RNA genome into DNA; **integrase** then inserts that DNA into a host chromosome as a provirus, which is replicated with every cell division. Reverse transcriptase is tempting, but it only makes the DNA and does not splice it in.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-016c'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'If mRNAs in an embryo became abnormally long-lived, which process would be most disrupted?',choices:['Cell differentiation during development','Steady housekeeping protein synthesis','Passive glucose uptake by cells','DNA replication fidelity'],ans:0,exp:'Short mRNA half-lives let cells switch proteins on and off quickly, and **development and differentiation** depend on precise timing of gene expression, so lingering mRNAs would keep old programs running. Housekeeping genes are expressed steadily, so longer mRNA life would barely affect them.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-017'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'Shortening of which mRNA feature typically triggers degradation of a eukaryotic transcript?',choices:['5\' cap','Kozak sequence','Poly-A tail','Intron branch point'],ans:2,exp:'The **poly-A tail** is gradually shortened in the cytoplasm; once it is short, the mRNA is decapped and degraded, and AU-rich elements in the 3\' UTR and microRNAs speed this up. The 5\' cap also protects the mRNA, but decapping usually follows, rather than starts, tail shortening.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-017b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'A fit person\'s heart and breathing rates rise in an exciting new setting and settle within days. What is the most likely cause?',choices:['Sympathetic nervous system activation','Parasympathetic nervous system activation','Hypoxia-driven chemoreceptor reflex','Increased thyroid hormone secretion'],ans:0,exp:'Novelty and arousal trigger the **sympathetic** fight-or-flight response: norepinephrine and epinephrine raise heart rate, contractility and ventilation, and the effect fades as the situation becomes familiar. Parasympathetic (vagal) activity does the opposite, slowing the heart.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-018'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'Which effect is produced by the parasympathetic nervous system?',choices:['Bronchodilation','Pupil dilation','Glucose release from the liver','Increased digestive motility'],ans:3,exp:'**Parasympathetic** (vagal, ACh) output promotes rest and digest: slower heart rate, bronchoconstriction, pupil constriction and stimulated digestion. Bronchodilation, pupil dilation and hepatic glucose release are all sympathetic effects.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-018b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'Which neurotransmitter do sympathetic postganglionic neurons release onto most target organs?',choices:['Acetylcholine','Norepinephrine','Epinephrine','Dopamine'],ans:1,exp:'All preganglionic neurons release ACh onto nicotinic receptors, but most **sympathetic postganglionic** neurons release **norepinephrine** (sweat glands are the cholinergic exception). Epinephrine is tempting, but it is released into the blood by the adrenal medulla, not by postganglionic nerve endings.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-018c'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Why do heart and breathing rates stay elevated during the first days at high altitude?',choices:['High PCO₂ stimulates central chemoreceptors','Low air pressure activates arterial baroreceptors','Cold air triggers hypothalamic shivering','Low PO₂ stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors'],ans:3,exp:'Lower atmospheric pressure means lower arterial **PO₂**, which the **carotid and aortic bodies** detect, driving faster breathing and heart rate until acclimatization catches up. Hyperventilation actually lowers PCO₂, so central CO₂ chemoreceptors are not the driver.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-019'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Over weeks at high altitude, which change increases the blood\'s oxygen-carrying capacity?',choices:['Decreased 2,3-BPG in red blood cells','Increased EPO secretion by the kidneys','Decreased resting ventilation rate','Increased plasma volume'],ans:1,exp:'Hypoxia stimulates the kidneys to release **erythropoietin (EPO)**, raising red cell and hemoglobin levels so each liter of blood carries more O₂. 2,3-BPG actually rises at altitude, lowering hemoglobin\'s affinity to help unload O₂ in tissues rather than raising carrying capacity.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-019b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Under normal sea-level conditions, what is the main chemical stimulus that sets breathing rate?',choices:['Arterial PO₂ sensed by carotid bodies','Blood glucose sensed by the hypothalamus','Arterial PCO₂/H⁺ sensed in the medulla','Blood lactate sensed by the pons'],ans:2,exp:'Normally **CO₂** (acting through H⁺ in cerebrospinal fluid) on **central chemoreceptors** in the medulla sets ventilation. Low PO₂ sensed by the peripheral carotid and aortic bodies becomes a major drive only when PO₂ falls substantially, as at high altitude.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-019c'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'How does a sustained rise in blood pressure affect urine output, and why?',choices:['Decreases it: more ADH and aldosterone','Increases it: more filtration and ANP, less ADH','Increases it: more renin and aldosterone','No change: autoregulation fully compensates'],ans:1,exp:'Higher arterial pressure raises filtration and tubular Na⁺ excretion (**pressure diuresis/natriuresis**), and the larger volume stretches the atria to release **ANP** while suppressing ADH. Autoregulation buffers GFR but does not fully cancel pressure natriuresis, a key long-term blood pressure control.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-020'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which hormone increases urine output?',choices:['Antidiuretic hormone','Aldosterone','Atrial natriuretic peptide','Angiotensin II'],ans:2,exp:'**ANP**, released from stretched atria, promotes Na⁺ and water excretion, raising urine output. ADH (aquaporin insertion in the collecting duct), aldosterone (Na⁺ reabsorption) and angiotensin II all conserve water; alcohol raises urine output by inhibiting ADH release.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-020b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Myoglobin appearing in the urine most likely indicates which injury?',choices:['Skeletal muscle breakdown','Red blood cell hemolysis','Bone fracture','Liver cell damage'],ans:0,exp:'Damaged muscle (**rhabdomyolysis**) releases myoglobin into the blood, where it is small enough to be filtered and can clog and injure kidney tubules. Hemolysis releases hemoglobin, not myoglobin, and bone contains no myoglobin.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-021'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'Compared with hemoglobin, myoglobin shows a _____ O₂-binding curve and _____ O₂ affinity',choices:['sigmoidal / higher','hyperbolic / lower','hyperbolic / higher','sigmoidal / lower'],ans:2,exp:'**Myoglobin** is a single subunit that stores O₂ in muscle, so it has no cooperativity (**hyperbolic** curve) and very high affinity. Hemoglobin\'s four subunits bind cooperatively, giving a sigmoidal curve and lower affinity that lets it unload O₂ in tissues.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-021b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'Heart rate, muscle coordination and appetite are controlled mainly by the _____, _____ and _____, respectively (order matters)',choices:['cerebellum / medulla / hypothalamus','medulla / hypothalamus / cerebellum','hypothalamus / cerebellum / medulla','medulla / cerebellum / hypothalamus'],ans:3,exp:'The **medulla** (brain stem) houses the cardiovascular centers, the **cerebellum** coordinates movement, and the **hypothalamus** regulates appetite, thirst and temperature. Swapping the cerebellum and medulla is the common trap.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-022'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'Which brain structure serves mainly as a relay station for incoming sensory signals to the cortex?',choices:['Thalamus','Hypothalamus','Pons','Cerebellum'],ans:0,exp:'The **thalamus** relays almost all sensory input (except smell) to the appropriate cortical areas. The hypothalamus sits just below it but controls homeostasis and the pituitary, not sensory relay.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-022b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'In prolonged cold, why do constricted skin blood vessels periodically dilate for brief intervals?',choices:['To dump excess core heat to the air','To deliver O₂ and prevent cold injury','To raise sweat gland output','To lower systemic blood pressure'],ans:1,exp:'Vasoconstriction conserves core heat, but **cold-induced vasodilation** periodically restores flow so skin tissue gets enough O₂ and warmth to avoid cold injury such as frostbite. It sacrifices a little heat rather than serving to release it.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-023'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which response helps defend core temperature in the cold?',choices:['Cutaneous vasodilation','Increased sweating','Brown fat thermogenesis','Lower thyroid hormone'],ans:2,exp:'In the cold the hypothalamus triggers skin vasoconstriction, shivering, piloerection and **non-shivering thermogenesis** (brown fat, thyroid hormone). Vasodilation and sweating are heat-loss responses used in hot conditions.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-023b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Why can a person eat more in a cold climate without gaining weight?',choices:['Cold reduces intestinal nutrient absorption','Cold suppresses insulin secretion','Cold increases urinary glucose loss','Extra calories are burned to make heat'],ans:3,exp:'Shivering, **non-shivering thermogenesis** and a higher metabolic rate burn the extra energy to generate heat, so intake matches expenditure. Cold does not meaningfully reduce nutrient absorption or cause glucose loss in urine.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-023c'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'A hand withdraws from a hot stove by spinal reflex, and pain is also felt. Where does the sensory signal branch to reach the brain?',choices:['At the neuromuscular junction','In the motor neuron axon','In the spinal cord, onto ascending neurons','Through synapses in the dorsal root ganglion'],ans:2,exp:'The reflex arc runs sensory neuron → spinal interneuron → motor neuron → muscle without the brain; **pain** is perceived because the sensory neuron also synapses in the **spinal cord** onto ascending tract neurons. The dorsal root ganglion holds sensory cell bodies but has no synapses, and motor terminals carry no sensory information.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-024'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'Afferent sensory neurons enter the spinal cord through the _____ root, with cell bodies located in the _____',choices:['dorsal / dorsal root ganglion','ventral / dorsal root ganglion','dorsal / ventral horn','ventral / ventral horn'],ans:0,exp:'**Afferent** (sensory) fibers carry signals toward the CNS through the **dorsal root**, and their cell bodies sit in the dorsal root ganglion. Efferent motor neurons have cell bodies in the ventral horn and exit through the ventral root.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-024b'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2A',q:'Which feature definitively identifies a cell as eukaryotic?',choices:['A rigid cell wall','A membrane-bound nucleus','Cytoplasmic ribosomes','Genetic exchange between cells'],ans:1,exp:'Only eukaryotes have **membrane-bound organelles** such as a nucleus, mitochondria, ER and Golgi. Bacteria also have cell walls and ribosomes and exchange DNA by conjugation, transformation and transduction.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-025'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2A',q:'Bacteria have _____ ribosomes and a cell wall made of _____',choices:['80S / peptidoglycan','70S / chitin','80S / cellulose','70S / peptidoglycan'],ans:3,exp:'Prokaryotes have **70S** ribosomes (30S + 50S) and walls of **peptidoglycan**. Eukaryotic ribosomes are 80S (40S + 60S); fungi build walls of chitin, plants of cellulose, and animal cells have none.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-025b'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Which event occurs in mitosis but NOT in meiosis I?',choices:['Separation of sister chromatids','Separation of homologous chromosomes','Crossing over between homologs','Formation of tetrads'],ans:0,exp:'In mitotic anaphase centromeres split and **sister chromatids** separate; in meiosis I homologs separate while sisters stay joined until meiosis II. Homolog separation, crossing over and tetrads are meiosis I events, not mitotic ones.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-026'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Which event occurs in meiosis I but not in mitosis?',choices:['Splitting of centromeres','Synapsis of homologous chromosomes','Alignment of chromosomes at the metaphase plate','Breakdown of the nuclear envelope'],ans:1,exp:'In prophase I homologs pair (**synapsis**) into tetrads and cross over, then separate in anaphase I, reducing 2n to n. Metaphase alignment and nuclear envelope breakdown happen in both divisions, and centromeres split in mitosis and meiosis II, not meiosis I.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-026b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which is NOT a function of mammalian skin?',choices:['Vitamin D synthesis','Thermoregulation','Respiratory gas exchange','Barrier against water loss'],ans:2,exp:'Mammalian skin protects against injury, infection and dehydration, senses touch and temperature, regulates heat and makes **vitamin D**, but **gas exchange** occurs in the lungs. Amphibians, by contrast, exchange significant gas through their moist skin.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-027'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which skin layer lacks blood vessels and contains keratinocytes and melanocytes?',choices:['Dermis','Hypodermis','Papillary dermis','Epidermis'],ans:3,exp:'The **epidermis** is avascular stratified epithelium of keratinocytes and melanocytes, nourished by diffusion from below. The dermis (including its papillary layer) holds blood vessels, sweat glands, hair follicles and sensory receptors, and the hypodermis is mainly insulating fat.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-027b'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Why does inbreeding tend to reduce fitness in a population?',choices:['It raises the mutation rate in offspring','It increases homozygosity for recessive alleles','It shifts allele frequencies toward harmful ones','It increases the frequency of heterozygotes'],ans:1,exp:'Inbreeding increases **homozygosity**, so deleterious recessive alleles hidden in heterozygotes are expressed more often (**inbreeding depression**). It changes genotype frequencies, not allele frequencies or the mutation rate.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-028'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Which Hardy-Weinberg assumption does inbreeding violate?',choices:['No mutation','No migration','Random mating','Large population size'],ans:2,exp:'Hardy-Weinberg (p + q = 1; p² + 2pq + q² = 1) assumes a large population, **random mating**, and no mutation, migration or selection. Inbreeding is nonrandom mating among relatives; by itself it shifts genotypes toward homozygotes without changing allele frequencies.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-028b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'What is the renal transport maximum (Tm) for a reabsorbed substance?',choices:['The plasma level at which filtration begins','The maximum rate it can be filtered','Its maximum concentration in the urine','The maximum rate carriers can reabsorb it'],ans:3,exp:'**Tm** is the reabsorption rate at which all tubule carriers are saturated; once the filtered load exceeds it, the excess spills into the urine. Filtration has no such maximum; it simply scales with GFR and plasma concentration.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-029'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which sequence correctly traces filtrate through the nephron?',choices:['Bowman\'s capsule → PCT → loop of Henle → DCT → collecting duct','Bowman\'s capsule → DCT → loop of Henle → PCT → collecting duct','Bowman\'s capsule → loop of Henle → PCT → DCT → collecting duct','Bowman\'s capsule → PCT → DCT → loop of Henle → collecting duct'],ans:0,exp:'Filtrate leaves the glomerulus into **Bowman\'s capsule**, then flows through the proximal convoluted tubule, the descending and ascending loop of Henle, the distal convoluted tubule and the collecting duct before draining to the renal pelvis, ureter and bladder. The proximal tubule always precedes the loop, and the distal tubule follows it.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-029b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Glucose is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule mainly by which mechanism?',choices:['Primary active transport by a glucose ATPase','Na⁺-coupled secondary active transport','Simple diffusion through the lipid bilayer','Paracellular flow between tubule cells'],ans:1,exp:'**SGLT** cotransporters move glucose into proximal tubule cells together with Na⁺, using the gradient made by the basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, so this is **secondary active transport**. No glucose-pumping ATPase exists; ATP is spent only indirectly by the Na⁺/K⁺ pump.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-029c'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'If the filtered load of glucose stays below its Tm, how much glucose appears in the urine?',choices:['About half of the filtered load','All of the filtered load','Essentially none','An amount proportional to GFR'],ans:2,exp:'Below **Tm** the carriers have spare capacity and reabsorb all filtered glucose, so urine glucose is essentially zero. **Glycosuria** appears only when the filtered load exceeds Tm, as in uncontrolled diabetes.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-030'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'For a substance that is filtered and partly reabsorbed but not secreted, how is the amount reabsorbed per minute calculated?',choices:['Filtered load minus excretion rate','Excretion rate minus filtered load','GFR divided by plasma concentration','Urine flow times plasma concentration'],ans:0,exp:'**Filtered load** = GFR × plasma concentration and **excretion rate** = urine concentration × urine flow, so reabsorption = filtered load − excretion. Subtracting the other way gives net secretion, which is negative for a reabsorbed substance.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-030b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'How does a fall in blood pressure affect renal clearance of a filtered, reabsorbed solute?',choices:['It increases because more is secreted','It is unchanged because Tm is fixed','It increases because urine flow rises','It decreases because less is filtered'],ans:3,exp:'Lower blood pressure reduces glomerular hydrostatic pressure and **GFR**, so less solute is filtered and a larger fraction of it can be reabsorbed, lowering **clearance**. A fixed Tm does not hold clearance constant, because clearance depends on how much solute reaches the carriers.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-031'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which substance gives an accurate measure of GFR because it is filtered but neither reabsorbed nor secreted?',choices:['Glucose','Inulin','Para-aminohippuric acid','Urea'],ans:1,exp:'**Inulin** clearance, (U × V)/P, equals GFR because it is freely filtered and none is reabsorbed or secreted; creatinine is a clinical approximation. PAH is both filtered and secreted, so its clearance estimates renal plasma flow instead.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-031b'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'A graph plots urine concentration against plasma concentration for two reabsorbed solutes. Solute X first appears in urine at a lower plasma concentration than solute Y. Assuming equal GFR, what can be concluded?',choices:['X has a higher Tm than Y','X is filtered less than Y','X has a lower Tm than Y','X is secreted while Y is not'],ans:2,exp:'The plasma concentration where a line leaves zero urine concentration is the **renal threshold**, where filtered load reaches **Tm**; at equal GFR, a lower threshold means a lower Tm. X therefore spills into urine sooner and is cleared more at any plasma concentration above its threshold.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-032'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Above roughly what plasma glucose concentration does glucose begin to appear in normal human urine?',choices:['About 100 mg/dL','About 180–200 mg/dL','About 70 mg/dL','About 400 mg/dL'],ans:1,exp:'The renal threshold for glucose is about **180–200 mg/dL**; below it, proximal tubule SGLT carriers reclaim all filtered glucose. 100 mg/dL is roughly normal fasting glucose, well below the threshold.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-033'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'A rise in arterial blood pressure most directly changes which renal variable?',choices:['Transport maximum for glucose','Collecting duct water permeability','Number of tubule carriers','Glomerular filtration rate'],ans:3,exp:'Higher arterial pressure raises glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure and therefore **GFR**. Tm depends on the number of tubule carriers, not pressure, and collecting duct water permeability is set by ADH.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-034'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'ADH is synthesized in the _____ and released from the _____',choices:['hypothalamus / posterior pituitary','hypothalamus / anterior pituitary','posterior pituitary / hypothalamus','anterior pituitary / posterior pituitary'],ans:0,exp:'ADH is made by **hypothalamic** neurons and travels down their axons to be released from the **posterior pituitary**; it inserts aquaporins in collecting ducts and at high doses constricts vessels (hence the name vasopressin). The anterior pituitary makes its own hormones under hypothalamic releasing factors, not ADH.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-034b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which force favors fluid movement out of the glomerular capillaries into Bowman\'s space?',choices:['Plasma oncotic pressure','Bowman\'s capsule hydrostatic pressure','Glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure','Renal venous pressure'],ans:2,exp:'**Glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure** pushes fluid out; Bowman\'s capsule hydrostatic pressure and plasma **oncotic** pressure from proteins oppose it, and net filtration is the difference. Plasma oncotic pressure pulls water back into the capillary rather than out of it.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-034c'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which experiment best shows that the endothelium is required for a blood vessel\'s relaxation response?',choices:['Compare vessels from two different species','Compare intact vessels with vessels lacking only endothelium','Test intact vessels at many drug doses','Compare endothelium-free vessels with skeletal muscle'],ans:1,exp:'To show **necessity**, remove only the layer in question and compare with an intact control under identical conditions; if the response disappears without it, the layer is required. A dose series on intact vessels alone has no comparison group lacking the layer.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-035'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Acetylcholine relaxes vascular smooth muscle mainly by which pathway?',choices:['Endothelial NO raising Ca²⁺ in smooth muscle','Smooth muscle nicotinic receptors causing depolarization','Endothelial NO raising cGMP in smooth muscle','Endothelial thromboxane raising IP₃ in smooth muscle'],ans:2,exp:'ACh binds endothelial muscarinic receptors, raising endothelial Ca²⁺ so **eNOS** makes NO from L-arginine; NO diffuses into smooth muscle, activates guanylyl cyclase, and **cGMP** causes relaxation. Ca²⁺ rises in the endothelium, not the smooth muscle, where a Ca²⁺ rise would cause contraction.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-035b'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'A response occurs at 10⁻⁷ M agonist when tissue X is present but not even at 10⁻⁶ M when it is absent. What is the strongest valid conclusion?',choices:['Tissue X raises sensitivity exactly 10-fold','Tissue X raises sensitivity at least 100-fold','Tissue X lowers sensitivity 10-fold','Tissue X raises sensitivity at least 10-fold'],ans:3,exp:'10⁻⁶ M is 10 times higher than 10⁻⁷ M, and the preparation without X still fails to respond there, so X increases sensitivity by **at least** one order of magnitude. Exactly 10-fold is unsupported, because the true threshold without X could be far higher.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-036'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'On a dose-response curve, where does a small change in agonist concentration produce the largest change in response?',choices:['At the steepest part, near the EC50','At the plateau near maximal response','At the lowest doses near baseline','At the highest dose tested'],ans:0,exp:'The tissue is most **sensitive** where the curve is **steepest**, typically around the EC50 (half-maximal response). At the plateau, receptors are saturated, so further concentration changes barely alter the response.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-037'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Mean arterial pressure is determined by the product of which two factors?',choices:['Heart rate and blood viscosity','Cardiac output and total peripheral resistance','Stroke volume and venous compliance','Cardiac output and total blood volume'],ans:1,exp:'**MAP = CO × TPR**, where cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume and resistance is set mostly by arteriole diameter. Blood volume affects MAP only indirectly, by changing venous return and thus cardiac output.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-038'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'If an arteriole\'s radius is halved, by about how much does its resistance increase?',choices:['2-fold','4-fold','8-fold','16-fold'],ans:3,exp:'By Poiseuille\'s law resistance ∝ 1/r⁴, so halving r raises resistance 2⁴ = **16-fold**. That steep dependence is why muscular **arterioles** dominate control of peripheral resistance; 4-fold would follow only from a 1/r² relationship.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-038b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'In which situation does widespread vasoconstriction benefit the body?',choices:['Vigorous aerobic exercise','Exposure to high heat','Hemorrhage with low blood volume','Emotional blushing'],ans:2,exp:'After **hemorrhage**, generalized vasoconstriction raises peripheral resistance to maintain blood pressure and redirect flow to the brain and heart. Exercise, heat exposure and blushing all rely on vasodilation in muscle or skin.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-039'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which response to hemorrhage occurs within seconds and is triggered by baroreceptors?',choices:['EPO-driven red blood cell production','Sympathetic tachycardia and vasoconstriction','Aldosterone-driven Na⁺ reabsorption','Vagal slowing of the heart rate'],ans:1,exp:'Falling pressure reduces firing of **baroreceptors** in the carotid sinus and aortic arch, rapidly boosting **sympathetic** output to raise heart rate and constrict vessels. The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and ADH conserve salt and water over minutes to hours, and EPO restores red cells over days.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-039b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'A blood vessel is kept relaxed by ongoing nitric oxide production. What happens if nitric oxide synthase is blocked?',choices:['Tension rises as smooth muscle contracts','Tension falls as smooth muscle relaxes','Tension is unchanged because NO is stored','Tension falls because cGMP accumulates'],ans:0,exp:'Blocking **NOS** stops NO production, so smooth muscle cGMP falls, the tonic relaxation is lost, and the vessel **constricts** with rising tension. NO is a gas made on demand, not stored, so there is no reserve to sustain relaxation.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-040'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'A competitive inhibitor _____ the apparent Km and _____ Vmax',choices:['increases / does not change','does not change / decreases','decreases / decreases','increases / decreases'],ans:0,exp:'A **competitive** inhibitor binds the active site, so more substrate is needed to reach half-maximal rate (Km up), but enough substrate can outcompete it (Vmax unchanged). Noncompetitive inhibitors lower Vmax with Km unchanged, and uncompetitive inhibitors lower both.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-040b'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'How does an uncoupler of oxidative phosphorylation affect mitochondria, leaving ATP-hungry cells such as sperm least able to function?',choices:['Blocks complex IV, so O₂ use and ATP both fall','Blocks ATP synthase, so O₂ use and ATP both fall','Lets H⁺ bypass ATP synthase, so O₂ use rises but ATP falls','Lets H⁺ bypass ATP synthase, so O₂ use and ATP both fall'],ans:2,exp:'An **uncoupler** (such as DNP, or thermogenin in brown fat) lets H⁺ leak back across the inner membrane without passing through ATP synthase, so electron transport speeds up, O₂ consumption rises and energy is released as **heat** instead of ATP. An ATP synthase blocker such as oligomycin instead slows O₂ use, because the unused proton gradient backs up the electron transport chain.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-041'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'Which pairing correctly names a natural uncoupler of oxidative phosphorylation and a chemical uncoupler, in that order?',choices:['Cytochrome c oxidase / 2,4-dinitrophenol','Thermogenin (UCP1) / oligomycin','Thermogenin (UCP1) / 2,4-dinitrophenol','Cytochrome c oxidase / cyanide'],ans:2,exp:'**Thermogenin (UCP1)** in brown fat lets protons re-enter the matrix without passing through ATP synthase, releasing the gradient energy as heat; **2,4-dinitrophenol** is a lipid-soluble weak acid that ferries protons across the inner membrane. Oligomycin is not an uncoupler: it blocks the ATP synthase proton channel, so electron transport slows rather than speeding up.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-041b'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'1D',q:'Why do lipid-soluble pesticides such as DDT reach their highest concentrations in top predators?',choices:['Stored in fat and excreted slowly, they concentrate at each trophic level','Being water-soluble, they are retained mainly in predator blood plasma','Rapid liver breakdown in prey converts them into more toxic products','Predators absorb them through membrane carriers that prey species lack'],ans:0,exp:'Hydrophobic toxins cross membranes easily, partition into adipose tissue and resist excretion, so each consumer retains what it eats over its lifetime and concentrations rise at each step up the food chain (**biomagnification**). Water-soluble compounds are the ones readily excreted in urine, so they do not accumulate this way.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-041c'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'A chemical applied to the skin causes burning and itching. What is its most likely direct effect on the neurons involved?',choices:['It hyperpolarizes sensory neurons, firing action potentials','It hyperpolarizes motor neurons, blocking action potentials','It depolarizes motor neurons, firing action potentials','It depolarizes sensory neurons, firing action potentials'],ans:3,exp:'Pain and itch are carried by **sensory (afferent) neurons**, so the chemical must depolarize them (for example, by increasing Na⁺ influx) to threshold so they fire. Hyperpolarization moves the membrane away from threshold and would make firing less likely.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-042'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'During the repolarization phase of a neuronal action potential, which ion movement predominates?',choices:['Na⁺ flows into the cell through voltage-gated channels','K⁺ flows out of the cell through voltage-gated channels','Na⁺ flows out of the cell through voltage-gated channels','K⁺ flows into the cell through voltage-gated channels'],ans:1,exp:'After the upstroke, Na⁺ channels inactivate and voltage-gated **K⁺ channels open**, so K⁺ leaves down its gradient and returns the membrane toward rest. Na⁺ influx drives depolarization, not repolarization; the slow closing of K⁺ channels then causes the brief hyperpolarization (undershoot).',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-042b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which of the following is a function of the liver?',choices:['Converting ammonia to urea','Secreting insulin and glucagon','Producing pepsinogen for protein digestion','Storing and concentrating bile'],ans:0,exp:'Hepatocytes run the **urea cycle**, turning toxic ammonia from amino acid breakdown into urea for renal excretion; the liver also makes bile and plasma proteins, stores glycogen and detoxifies drugs via cytochrome P450. The liver makes bile, but the gallbladder is what stores and concentrates it.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-043'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Damage to which cellular structure directly produces mutations that can initiate cancer?',choices:['Ribosomes','Golgi apparatus','Chromosomes','Plasma membrane'],ans:2,exp:'A mutation is a heritable change in DNA sequence, and the DNA is carried on **chromosomes**; mutations in proto-oncogenes or tumor suppressor genes can drive cancer. Ribosome damage impairs protein synthesis but is not passed to daughter cells as a sequence change.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-044'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Which statement correctly contrasts proto-oncogenes with tumor suppressor genes in cancer development?',choices:['Proto-oncogenes need loss of both alleles; tumor suppressors need one gain-of-function allele','Proto-oncogenes need one gain-of-function allele; tumor suppressors usually need loss of both alleles','Both gene types usually require loss-of-function mutations in both alleles','Both gene types usually require a single gain-of-function mutation in one allele'],ans:1,exp:'A proto-oncogene (e.g., **Ras**) promotes growth, so one activating mutation acts dominantly to create an oncogene. Tumor suppressors (e.g., **p53, Rb**) restrain growth, so typically both copies must be lost (Knudson two-hit hypothesis); the reversed pairing is the classic trap.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-044b'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'Why does an enzyme lose activity when the pH moves far from its optimum?',choices:['Extreme pH hydrolyzes the peptide bonds of the enzyme backbone','Extreme pH raises the activation energy of the uncatalyzed reaction','Excess H⁺ ions bind the active site as competitive inhibitors','Altered side-chain charges disrupt bonds holding the active site shape'],ans:3,exp:'Protonating or deprotonating ionizable side chains (Asp, Glu, His, Lys) changes their charge, breaking the salt bridges and hydrogen bonds that maintain tertiary structure and the active site, a form of **denaturation**. The peptide bonds of the primary structure stay intact; only noncovalent interactions are disrupted.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-045'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'1A',q:'Pepsinogen is activated by _____, and trypsinogen is activated by _____.',choices:['HCl / enteropeptidase','enteropeptidase / HCl','HCl / bicarbonate','bile salts / enteropeptidase'],ans:0,exp:'Chief cells secrete **pepsinogen**, which stomach HCl (low pH) converts to pepsin, active near pH 1.5–2. Pancreatic trypsinogen is activated in the duodenum by the brush-border enzyme **enteropeptidase**, and trypsin works best near pH 8; reversing the pairing is the common error.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-045b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'If plasma albumin concentration is raised, which way will fluid tend to move across systemic capillaries?',choices:['From the blood into the tissues','From the blood into the lymphatics','From the tissues into the blood','No net movement in either direction'],ans:2,exp:'Albumin cannot cross capillary walls, so raising it increases plasma **oncotic pressure**, which draws water osmotically from the interstitium into the blood. Low albumin (liver failure, nephrotic syndrome) does the opposite and causes edema.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-046'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'According to the classic Starling model, what happens at the venous end of a systemic capillary?',choices:['Hydrostatic pressure exceeds oncotic pressure, so fluid filters out','Oncotic pressure exceeds hydrostatic pressure, so fluid is reabsorbed','Oncotic pressure exceeds hydrostatic pressure, so fluid filters out','Hydrostatic pressure exceeds oncotic pressure, so fluid is reabsorbed'],ans:1,exp:'Blood hydrostatic pressure falls along the capillary while plasma oncotic pressure stays roughly constant, so at the venous end **oncotic pressure dominates** and fluid returns. Hydrostatic pressure dominating describes the arterial end, and the small excess filtrate is returned by lymphatics.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-046b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'A person eats a very high-salt diet for several days. How does aldosterone secretion change?',choices:['It decreases, so less Na⁺ is reabsorbed in the distal nephron','It increases, so more Na⁺ is reabsorbed in the distal nephron','It decreases, so more Na⁺ is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule','It increases, so more Na⁺ is excreted in the collecting duct'],ans:0,exp:'Aldosterone increases Na⁺ reabsorption (and K⁺ secretion) in the distal tubule and collecting duct, so with excess Na⁺ the renin-angiotensin system is suppressed and **aldosterone falls**, letting the kidney excrete salt. Expanded volume also raises ANP, which promotes natriuresis; a rise in aldosterone would worsen the salt load.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-047'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'In the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, which reaction does ACE, found mainly on lung capillary endothelium, catalyze?',choices:['Angiotensinogen → angiotensin I','Angiotensin I → angiotensin II','Cholesterol → aldosterone','Angiotensin II → angiotensin III'],ans:1,exp:'Renin from juxtaglomerular cells cleaves angiotensinogen to angiotensin I; **ACE** then converts angiotensin I to the active **angiotensin II**, which constricts arterioles and stimulates aldosterone, ADH and thirst. The angiotensinogen step is the job of renin, not ACE, and aldosterone is made from cholesterol in the adrenal cortex.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-047b'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'After partial removal of the liver, how is the lost tissue mainly regenerated?',choices:['Meiosis of mature hepatocytes','Binary fission of mature hepatocytes','Fusion of mature hepatocytes','Mitosis of mature hepatocytes'],ans:3,exp:'Hepatocytes are **stable** cells resting in G0; after injury they re-enter the cell cycle and divide by **mitosis**, producing genetically identical diploid cells. Meiosis only produces haploid gametes, and binary fission is the prokaryotic division mechanism.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-048'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'Which tissue is made of permanent cells that rarely, if ever, divide in adults?',choices:['Intestinal epithelium','Liver parenchyma','Cardiac muscle','Bone marrow'],ans:2,exp:'Neurons and **cardiac myocytes** are permanent cells, so damage heals by scarring rather than regeneration. Liver is the tempting choice, but hepatocytes are stable cells that leave G0 and divide when needed; gut lining and marrow are labile, constantly dividing tissues.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-048b'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'Why does mitotic cyclin concentration fall sharply as a cell exits mitosis?',choices:['It is tagged with ubiquitin and degraded by proteasomes','Its gene is transcribed only during G1 phase','It is diluted as the cell divides into two','It is exported from the nucleus into the cytoplasm'],ans:0,exp:'Cyclin accumulates through interphase, activates CDKs at the start of mitosis, and is then ubiquitinated by the **APC/C** for proteasomal destruction, which inactivates the CDK and lets the cell finish dividing. Dilution by cell division would only halve the level, not produce the abrupt drop seen each cycle.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-049'},
  {s:'Cells, Membranes & Transport',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'How do cyclin-dependent kinases (CDKs) differ from cyclins across the cell cycle?',choices:['CDK levels oscillate, while cyclins are present at constant levels','Both CDKs and cyclins are degraded at the end of each phase','CDK levels stay roughly constant, while cyclin levels oscillate','CDKs are active alone, while cyclins act as their inhibitors'],ans:2,exp:'CDKs are present throughout the cycle but are **inactive without a cyclin partner**; cyclins are synthesized and destroyed in waves, so each cyclin-CDK complex is active only in its phase. Reversing which component oscillates is the usual error.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-049b'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'Which treatment best prevents recurrence of peptic ulcers caused by Helicobacter pylori?',choices:['A proton pump inhibitor alone','Antibiotics plus a proton pump inhibitor','Antacids plus an H2 blocker','An H2 blocker alone'],ans:1,exp:'Antibiotics eradicate **H. pylori**, removing the cause, while acid suppression lets the mucosa heal; standard therapy combines both. Acid suppression alone heals the ulcer temporarily, but the bacterium persists and the ulcer returns.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-050'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'Which enzyme allows Helicobacter pylori to survive in the acidic stomach?',choices:['Hyaluronidase','Coagulase','Lipase','Urease'],ans:3,exp:'**Urease** converts urea into ammonia and CO₂, and the ammonia neutralizes acid around the bacterium as it burrows into the mucus layer near the epithelium; this is the basis of the urea breath test. Coagulase is a clotting enzyme of Staphylococcus aureus and plays no role in acid survival.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-050b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'Which gastric cells secrete both HCl and intrinsic factor?',choices:['Parietal cells','Chief cells','G cells','Mucous neck cells'],ans:0,exp:'**Parietal cells** pump H⁺ via the H⁺/K⁺-ATPase and secrete intrinsic factor, which is needed for vitamin B12 absorption in the ileum. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, G cells secrete gastrin, and mucous cells secrete mucus and bicarbonate.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-050c'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'Which finding provides the strongest evidence that a specific microbe causes a disease?',choices:['Patients with the disease have antibodies against the microbe','The microbe is more common in sick people than in healthy people','The pure-cultured microbe causes the disease in a healthy host','The microbe is found in the environment where patients live'],ans:2,exp:'Under **Koch\'s postulates**, the microbe must be present in all cases, grown in pure culture, cause the disease when introduced into a healthy host, and be re-isolated from that host; the inoculation step establishes causation. Antibodies only show exposure, and higher prevalence in patients only shows association.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-051'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'For one bacterial strain, the growth curves with and without an antibiotic overlap. What is the best conclusion?',choices:['The strain is sensitive to the antibiotic','The strain is resistant to the antibiotic','The antibiotic accelerates growth of the strain','The strain has entered its death phase'],ans:1,exp:'If treatment does not change the curve, the drug is not affecting growth, so the strain is **resistant**. A sensitive strain would show a treated curve that plateaus or falls below the untreated one; a steeper exponential slope would indicate faster growth.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-052'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'In a batch bacterial culture, which growth phase shows a stable population because nutrients limit growth and division balances death?',choices:['Lag phase','Exponential phase','Death phase','Stationary phase'],ans:3,exp:'In **stationary phase**, nutrient depletion and waste buildup slow division until births equal deaths, so the count plateaus. Lag phase also looks flat on a graph, but there cells are adjusting to new medium before dividing, not limited by depleted nutrients.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-052b'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'Streptomycin selectively harms bacteria by binding which target?',choices:['The 30S ribosomal subunit','The 50S ribosomal subunit','Peptidoglycan cross-linking enzymes','Bacterial DNA gyrase'],ans:0,exp:'Aminoglycosides such as **streptomycin** (and tetracyclines) bind the bacterial **30S** subunit and disrupt translation, while human cytoplasmic ribosomes are 80S, which gives selectivity. Macrolides and chloramphenicol target the 50S subunit instead, and penicillins block peptidoglycan synthesis.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-052c'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'2B',q:'Which ATP-producing feature is shared by aerobic bacteria and human cells?',choices:['ATP synthase located on the outer mitochondrial membrane','Electron transport carried out on the nuclear envelope','A membrane proton gradient that drives ATP synthase','Glycolysis carried out inside membrane-bound organelles'],ans:2,exp:'Both use **chemiosmosis**: an electron transport chain pumps H⁺ across a membrane, and ATP synthase uses that gradient to make ATP. In bacteria this happens on the plasma membrane, and in humans on the inner (not outer) mitochondrial membrane.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-053'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'Which feature is typical of bacterial DNA organization but not of human nuclear DNA?',choices:['Multiple linear chromosomes','A single circular chromosome in a nucleoid','DNA wrapped around histone octamers','Chromosomes enclosed by a nuclear envelope'],ans:1,exp:'Bacteria usually have one **circular chromosome** in the nucleoid region, often plus plasmids, and generally lack histones. Humans have multiple linear, histone-wrapped chromosomes inside a nucleus, although their mitochondrial DNA is circular, like that of the bacterial ancestors of mitochondria.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-053b'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'1A',q:'Which experiment best tests whether enzyme X is necessary for a bacterial process?',choices:['Measure enzyme X levels in cells while the process occurs','Add extra enzyme X to cells and look for a faster process','Show that enzyme X levels rise and fall with the process','Delete the gene for enzyme X and see if the process still occurs'],ans:3,exp:'Necessity is shown by **removing the factor** (a knockout) in an otherwise normal system: if the outcome disappears, X is required. Measuring or tracking X alongside the process only shows correlation, and adding extra X tests sufficiency or rate, not necessity.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-054'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'What is the underlying cause of rickets?',choices:['Insufficient vitamin D activity, so bone is poorly mineralized','Excess parathyroid hormone, so bone is overly resorbed','Insufficient vitamin C, so collagen is poorly cross-linked','Excess calcitonin, so osteoclasts are overly inhibited'],ans:0,exp:'Rickets results from too little **vitamin D** action (deficiency, failed activation to calcitriol, or unresponsive tissue), which cuts intestinal Ca²⁺ absorption so new bone matrix mineralizes poorly and long bones bow. Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy, a collagen defect rather than a mineralization defect.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-055'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which sequence of organs correctly traces the activation of vitamin D?',choices:['Skin → kidney → liver','Liver → skin → kidney','Skin → liver → kidney','Kidney → liver → skin'],ans:2,exp:'UV light converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in the **skin** to cholecalciferol, the **liver** adds the 25-hydroxyl group, and kidney 1α-hydroxylase (stimulated by PTH) forms the active calcitriol, 1,25-(OH)₂D. Swapping the liver and kidney steps is the common mistake; the kidney performs the final, regulated activation.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-055b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'How does the bone mineral-to-matrix ratio compare in rickets and in osteoporosis, respectively?',choices:['Normal in rickets / low in osteoporosis','Low in rickets / normal in osteoporosis','Low in rickets / low in osteoporosis','High in rickets / normal in osteoporosis'],ans:1,exp:'In rickets and osteomalacia, matrix is laid down but poorly mineralized, so the **mineral-to-matrix ratio falls**. In osteoporosis, resorption outpaces formation and total bone mass drops, but the bone that remains is normally mineralized.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-055c'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Why are calcium supplements often combined with vitamin D?',choices:['Vitamin D blocks calcium excretion by the liver','Vitamin D stimulates osteoclasts to store calcium in bone','Vitamin D lowers parathyroid hormone to prevent hypercalcemia','Vitamin D increases calcium absorption in the small intestine'],ans:3,exp:'Active vitamin D (**calcitriol**) induces intestinal calcium transport proteins, so more dietary calcium enters the blood; without it, much of the supplement passes unabsorbed. Calcium is not excreted by the liver, and osteoclasts release rather than store calcium.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-056'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Blood calcium falls below normal. Which hormonal response restores it?',choices:['PTH rises, increasing bone resorption and renal Ca²⁺ reabsorption','Calcitonin rises, increasing bone resorption and renal Ca²⁺ reabsorption','PTH falls, decreasing bone resorption and renal Ca²⁺ excretion','Calcitonin falls, increasing osteoblast deposition of Ca²⁺ in bone'],ans:0,exp:'Low Ca²⁺ triggers **parathyroid hormone**, which increases osteoclast-mediated bone resorption, boosts renal Ca²⁺ reabsorption and stimulates kidney activation of vitamin D for gut absorption. Calcitonin acts in the opposite direction, rising when calcium is high and inhibiting osteoclasts.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-057'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which bone cells resorb bone matrix and release calcium into the blood?',choices:['Osteoblasts','Osteocytes','Osteoclasts','Chondrocytes'],ans:2,exp:'**Osteoclasts** are multinucleated cells that dissolve mineral and digest matrix (mnemonic: osteoClasts Chew bone). Osteoblasts do the opposite, laying down matrix and depositing calcium, and become osteocytes once embedded in it.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-057b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'Under what plasma calcium condition is calcitonin secretion increased, and what is its main effect?',choices:['Low plasma calcium / stimulates osteoclasts','High plasma calcium / inhibits osteoclasts','High plasma calcium / stimulates osteoclasts','Low plasma calcium / inhibits osteoclasts'],ans:1,exp:'Thyroid parafollicular C cells release **calcitonin** when plasma calcium is high; it inhibits osteoclasts, reducing bone resorption and lowering blood calcium. Its effect in adults is modest, and it opposes PTH, which rises when calcium is low.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-058'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'What is the most dangerous acute consequence of surgically removing all parathyroid glands?',choices:['Hypercalcemia causing kidney stones','Hypercalcemia causing muscle weakness','Hypocalcemia causing flaccid paralysis','Hypocalcemia causing tetany'],ans:3,exp:'Without PTH, plasma Ca²⁺ falls, and low extracellular calcium lowers the threshold for Na⁺ channel opening, so nerves and muscles become **overexcitable**: tingling, spasms and **tetany**, including potentially fatal laryngospasm. Flaccid paralysis is the tempting wrong pairing, because hypocalcemia increases rather than decreases excitability.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-059'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'In skeletal muscle, which protein does calcium bind to expose the myosin-binding sites on actin?',choices:['Troponin','Tropomyosin','Calmodulin','Titin'],ans:0,exp:'Ca²⁺ released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds **troponin** (troponin C), which shifts tropomyosin off the myosin-binding sites. Tropomyosin is the blocking strand that moves, not the Ca²⁺ sensor; calmodulin plays the sensor role in smooth muscle.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-059b'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Several related lizard species use the UV reflectance of a throat patch as a mating signal. Which species would lose the least if it lost the ability to detect UV light?',choices:['The species whose patch reflects the most UV','The species living in the brightest habitat','The species whose patch reflects the least UV','The species with the largest throat patch'],ans:2,exp:'A species whose signal contains **little UV** depends least on UV detection, so losing that ability costs it the least. Picking the strongest reflector reverses the logic; on a reflectance graph, compare species specifically at the UV wavelengths.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-060'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'A trait appears only in males of a species. What can be concluded about the location of its gene?',choices:['It must be on the Y chromosome','It could be on an autosome or either sex chromosome','It must be on the X chromosome','It must be in the mitochondrial genome'],ans:1,exp:'**Sex-limited** traits can be encoded anywhere in the genome, with sex hormones controlling whether they are expressed. Only Y-linked genes are guaranteed to pass strictly from father to son, so male-only expression alone does not prove Y linkage.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-061'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Male-pattern baldness is coded by an autosomal gene but is expressed far more often in males. What type of trait is it?',choices:['Sex-linked','Sex-limited','Y-linked','Sex-influenced'],ans:3,exp:'**Sex-influenced** traits are autosomal but expressed differently depending on sex, here because androgens make the allele behave as dominant in males. Sex-limited traits appear in only one sex (e.g., milk production), and sex-linked traits are carried on the X or Y.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-061b'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'A man affected by an X-linked recessive disorder has children with a woman who is not a carrier. What is expected among their children?',choices:['All daughters are carriers, and no sons are affected','Half the sons are affected, and half the daughters are carriers','All sons are affected, and no daughters are carriers','All daughters are affected, and all sons are carriers'],ans:0,exp:'The father gives his **X** to every daughter, making them all carriers, and his Y to every son, so sons get their X from the noncarrier mother and are unaffected. Half of sons affected is the pattern for a carrier mother, not an affected father.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-061c'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Under the biological species concept, what makes two populations separate species?',choices:['They occupy different geographic habitats','They differ clearly in body form and color','They cannot produce fertile offspring together','They differ in DNA sequence by a set amount'],ans:2,exp:'The biological species concept defines species by **reproductive isolation**: the inability to interbreed in nature and produce viable, fertile offspring. Different habitats or appearances alone do not qualify, since such populations may still interbreed freely.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-062'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Horses and donkeys can mate, but their mule offspring are sterile. Which isolating mechanism does this illustrate?',choices:['Gametic isolation','Hybrid sterility','Hybrid inviability','Behavioral isolation'],ans:1,exp:'**Hybrid sterility** is a postzygotic barrier: the hybrid develops and survives but cannot reproduce. Hybrid inviability would mean the mule failed to develop or survive, and gametic and behavioral isolation are prezygotic barriers that act before or at fertilization.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-062b'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Survey data show that lizard species in bright, open habitats have greater UV reflectance than species in shaded habitats. Which conclusion do these data support?',choices:['High UV reflectance causes species to choose open habitats','Shaded habitats cause species to lose UV reflectance','UV reflectance determines which habitat a species survives in','UV reflectance is associated with open, bright habitats'],ans:3,exp:'Correlational data support only an **association** between the trait and habitat type. The other claims assert a direction of causation, which would require experimental manipulation that survey data do not provide.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-063'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'For a trait to increase in frequency by natural selection, which condition is essential?',choices:['It is heritable and increases reproductive success','It improves the survival of the individual','It improves communication among group members','It arises in response to an environmental need'],ans:0,exp:'Selection acts on **heritable variation** that raises fitness, meaning more surviving offspring. Better survival helps only insofar as it yields more offspring, and traits do not arise because they are needed (a Lamarckian misconception).',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-064'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Which type of selection favors both extremes of a trait over the intermediate form?',choices:['Directional selection','Stabilizing selection','Disruptive selection','Sexual selection'],ans:2,exp:'**Disruptive selection** favors both tails of the distribution and can split a population into two forms. Stabilizing selection is the opposite, favoring the average, while directional selection favors only one extreme.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-064b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'How does aldosterone deficiency affect blood volume and plasma potassium, respectively?',choices:['Volume rises / potassium falls','Volume falls / potassium rises','Volume falls / potassium falls','Volume rises / potassium rises'],ans:1,exp:'Without aldosterone, less Na⁺ is reabsorbed in the distal nephron, water follows the lost Na⁺, and blood volume and pressure drop; K⁺ secretion also falls, causing **hyperkalemia**, as in Addison disease. Hypokalemia occurs with aldosterone excess, not deficiency.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-065'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which sequence correctly orders the glands that drive the menstrual cycle, from the first signal to the final hormone output?',choices:['Anterior pituitary → hypothalamus → ovaries','Ovaries → anterior pituitary → hypothalamus','Hypothalamus → posterior pituitary → ovaries','Hypothalamus → anterior pituitary → ovaries'],ans:3,exp:'The **hypothalamus** releases GnRH, the **anterior pituitary** responds with FSH and LH, and the **ovaries** secrete estrogen and progesterone, which feed back on the first two. The posterior pituitary releases ADH and oxytocin, not gonadotropins.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-066'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'What directly triggers ovulation in the menstrual cycle?',choices:['An LH surge caused by estrogen positive feedback','An FSH drop caused by progesterone negative feedback','A progesterone surge from the corpus luteum','A fall in estrogen at the end of the follicular phase'],ans:0,exp:'High, sustained estrogen from the dominant follicle switches to **positive feedback**, producing the LH surge that triggers ovulation. Progesterone comes from the corpus luteum, which forms only after ovulation.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-066b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'If pregnancy occurs, which hormone maintains the corpus luteum during early pregnancy?',choices:['Luteinizing hormone','Follicle-stimulating hormone','Human chorionic gonadotropin','Prolactin'],ans:2,exp:'The trophoblast secretes **hCG**, which mimics LH and keeps the corpus luteum producing progesterone until the placenta takes over. Maternal LH falls after ovulation because progesterone suppresses it, so it cannot sustain the corpus luteum.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-066c'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'Double-stranded DNA is replicated in vitro using a nucleotide mix with excess dATP and dTTP. How does the (A+T)/(G+C) ratio of the daughter helices compare with the parent?',choices:['Higher than the parent DNA','The same as the parent DNA','Lower than the parent DNA','Equal to the ratio of nucleotides supplied'],ans:1,exp:'Base composition is set by the **template** through complementary base pairing, so daughter helices match the parent ratio regardless of nucleotide proportions. Excess dATP and dTTP can affect speed or fidelity but cannot dictate which base is added opposite each template base.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-067'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'A double-stranded DNA sample contains 30% adenine. What percentage of its bases are guanine?',choices:['30%','40%','70%','20%'],ans:3,exp:'By **Chargaff\'s rules**, A = T and G = C, so A + T = 60% and the remaining 40% splits equally between G and C, giving 20% guanine. Choosing 40% gives the combined G + C rather than guanine alone.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-067b'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'Which enzyme seals the nicks between Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand during DNA replication?',choices:['DNA ligase','DNA polymerase I','Primase','Topoisomerase'],ans:0,exp:'**DNA ligase** forms the final phosphodiester bond that joins adjacent Okazaki fragments. DNA polymerase I is the tempting choice: it removes RNA primers and fills the gaps with DNA, but it cannot seal the remaining nick.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-067c'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'In a eukaryotic cell, where and when is uracil, supplied as UTP, mainly incorporated into nucleic acid?',choices:['In the nucleus, during DNA replication','In the cytoplasm, during translation','In the nucleus, during transcription','In the cytoplasm, during reverse transcription'],ans:2,exp:'RNA polymerase uses UTP to build RNA during **transcription**, which occurs mainly in the nucleus (and to a lesser extent in mitochondria). DNA replication uses dTTP instead, and translation reads RNA rather than adding nucleotides to it.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-068'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'Which germ layer gives rise to skeletal muscle, bone and the kidneys?',choices:['Ectoderm','Mesoderm','Endoderm','Neural crest'],ans:1,exp:'**Mesoderm** forms muscle, bone, connective tissue, blood and vessels, kidneys, gonads and dermis. Ectoderm forms the nervous system and epidermis, and endoderm forms the gut lining and its derivatives, such as the liver, pancreas and lungs.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-069'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'Which structure is derived from neural crest cells?',choices:['Adrenal cortex','Spinal cord','Thyroid follicles','Adrenal medulla'],ans:3,exp:'Migratory **neural crest** cells form peripheral neurons and glia, melanocytes, some facial bone and cartilage, and the adrenal medulla, whose chromaffin cells act as modified sympathetic neurons. The adrenal cortex is mesodermal, and the spinal cord develops from the neural tube itself.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-069b'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'1D',q:'According to endosymbiotic theory, what capability did engulfed aerobic bacteria give an anaerobic host cell?',choices:['Oxidative phosphorylation using oxygen','Glycolysis in the cytoplasm','Mitotic cell division','Transcription and translation'],ans:0,exp:'The ancestors of mitochondria brought the **Krebs cycle, electron transport and oxidative phosphorylation**, yielding far more ATP per glucose. The host already performed glycolysis, which is why that pathway still runs in the cytoplasm.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-070'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'In a eukaryotic cell, where does the Krebs cycle take place?',choices:['Cytoplasm','Mitochondrial matrix','Inner mitochondrial membrane','Intermembrane space'],ans:1,exp:'Pyruvate dehydrogenase and the **Krebs cycle** run in the mitochondrial matrix. Electron transport and ATP synthase sit in the inner mitochondrial membrane, and glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-070b'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Most proteins found in mitochondria are encoded by nuclear genes. How is this observation best reconciled with the endosymbiotic theory?',choices:['Mitochondria arose from infoldings of the host cell membrane','Mitochondrial proteins evolved independently in the host nucleus','Many symbiont genes were transferred to the host nucleus over time','Mitochondria import ancestral bacterial proteins from outside the cell'],ans:2,exp:'Through **endosymbiotic gene transfer**, many genes of the ancestral bacterium moved to the host nucleus; their proteins are now made on cytoplasmic ribosomes and imported into the mitochondrion. The observation does not favor a membrane-infolding origin, because mitochondria still keep their own circular genome and bacteria-like ribosomes.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-071'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'A channel that lets protons leak freely across the inner mitochondrial membrane would cause ATP synthesis to _____ while electron transport _____.',choices:['fall / speeds up','fall / stops','rise / speeds up','stay constant / slows down'],ans:0,exp:'Proton leakage collapses the gradient, so no **proton-motive force** drives ATP synthase and ATP output drops. This is **uncoupling**: with no gradient to pump against, the electron transport chain runs faster and O₂ consumption rises, releasing the energy as heat rather than stopping.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-072'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'During chemiosmosis, electron transport complexes pump H⁺ from the _____ into the _____, and H⁺ then returns through ATP synthase.',choices:['intermembrane space / matrix','matrix / intermembrane space','cytosol / matrix','matrix / cytosol'],ans:1,exp:'ETC complexes pump H⁺ out of the **matrix** into the **intermembrane space**, making that space acidic and positively charged. Protons then flow down their gradient back into the matrix through ATP synthase; reversing the two compartments is the common mix-up.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-072b'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'Approximately how many ATP are produced for each NADH and each FADH₂ oxidized by the electron transport chain?',choices:['3 per NADH / 2 per FADH₂','2 per NADH / 2.5 per FADH₂','1.5 per NADH / 2.5 per FADH₂','2.5 per NADH / 1.5 per FADH₂'],ans:3,exp:'Current estimates are about **2.5 ATP per NADH** and **1.5 per FADH₂**, because FADH₂ donates electrons at complex II and bypasses the proton pumping of complex I. The older 3 and 2 values assumed whole-number stoichiometry; in both cases O₂ is the final electron acceptor, reduced to water.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-072c'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2B',q:'Which feature of mitochondria is evidence that they descended from bacteria?',choices:['Circular, histone-free DNA','Linear chromosomes with telomeres','Cytosolic-type 80S ribosomes','Division by spindle-based mitosis'],ans:0,exp:'Mitochondria have **circular, histone-free DNA** like bacteria, along with bacteria-like ribosomes, division by binary fission and a double membrane. They do not use cytosolic 80S ribosomes, which is why some antibiotics that target bacterial ribosomes also inhibit mitochondrial translation.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-073'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'Transporting K⁺ into the mitochondrial matrix reduces ATP synthesis even when matrix pH is unchanged. Why?',choices:['It inhibits ATP synthase by binding its catalytic site','It raises the matrix pH and removes the H⁺ gradient','It dissipates the electrical component of the proton-motive force','It blocks electron flow between complexes III and IV'],ans:2,exp:'The **proton-motive force** has two parts: a pH gradient and a membrane potential with the matrix negative. Moving positive K⁺ into the matrix reduces that charge difference, weakening the pull on H⁺ to enter through ATP synthase even though the pH gradient remains.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-074'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'An X-linked variant causes carrier males to father only daughters. If it spreads to fixation with no opposing selection, what is the expected outcome?',choices:['A stable 1:1 sex ratio is restored','The population goes extinct for lack of males','Females become rare and males predominate','The variant is lost through genetic drift'],ans:1,exp:'Once every male carries the driving X, every mating yields only daughters, so no males are born and the population **goes extinct**. Nothing restores a 1:1 ratio, because at fixation no standard X chromosomes remain to produce sons.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-075'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'An allele that is transmitted to more than 50% of a heterozygote\'s gametes, and spreads even when it harms the organism, is an example of:',choices:['balancing selection','genetic hitchhiking','heterozygote advantage','meiotic drive'],ans:3,exp:'**Meiotic drive** describes selfish genetic elements, such as sex-ratio distorters, that bias their own transmission above the Mendelian 50%. Balancing selection and heterozygote advantage maintain alleles because they raise fitness, whereas a driving allele can spread despite lowering it.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-075b'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'In a population, 40% of mothers are homozygous and 20% are heterozygous for an X-linked allele. What fraction of sons inherit the allele?',choices:['0.50','0.40','0.60','0.30'],ans:0,exp:'A son always receives his X from his **mother** (the father contributes Y), so the allele frequency in sons equals its frequency among maternal X chromosomes: 0.40 + ½(0.20) = **0.50**. Adding the full heterozygote fraction to get 0.60 ignores that heterozygous mothers pass the allele to only half their sons.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-076'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Why can a driving X chromosome spread through a population even though it provides no fitness benefit?',choices:['Carrier males produce more offspring than non-carriers','Carrier males pass it to all offspring rather than half','Carrier females are shielded from recessive mutations','Carrier females undergo more meiotic recombination'],ans:1,exp:'A normal male passes his X to only half his offspring (his daughters), but a carrier of the driving X has only daughters, so the driver reaches **all** of his offspring. This transmission advantage alone raises its frequency each generation; total offspring number does not need to increase.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-077'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'In a fly population, males carrying an X-linked sex-ratio distorter sire only daughters. A couple has several sons, and only some sons inherit an X-linked trait from the mother. The father _____ the distorter, and the mother is _____ for the trait allele.',choices:['carries / heterozygous','lacks / homozygous','lacks / heterozygous','carries / homozygous'],ans:2,exp:'A distorter-carrying father produces only daughters, so having **any sons** shows he has a normal X. Because sons get their X only from the mother, a mix of affected and unaffected sons means she is **heterozygous**; a homozygous mother would pass the allele to every son.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-078'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'An XO individual develops as a _____ in Drosophila and as a _____ in humans.',choices:['fertile male / sterile male','female / male','sterile female / female','sterile male / female'],ans:3,exp:'Drosophila sex is set by the **X-to-autosome ratio**, so XO flies are male, but they are sterile because the Y carries fertility genes. In humans the Y-linked **SRY** gene determines maleness, so XO individuals (Turner syndrome) are female.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-078b'},
  {s:'Evolution & Microbiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'1C',q:'Which condition would most likely prevent a driving allele from reaching fixation?',choices:['Non-carrier homozygotes have the highest fitness','All genotypes have equal fitness','Carriers have slightly higher fitness than non-carriers','The population is very large and randomly mating'],ans:0,exp:'Drive alone pushes an allele toward 100%, so only opposing **selection**, such as higher fitness in individuals that lack it, can hold it back. With equal fitness for all genotypes nothing counters the transmission advantage, and a large random-mating population merely removes drift as a factor.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-079'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'Which type of chemical messenger travels through the bloodstream to coordinate distant organs such as the brain, gut and adipose tissue?',choices:['Neurotransmitters','Hormones','Enzymes','Receptors'],ans:1,exp:'**Hormones** are endocrine signals secreted into the blood, letting one organ influence distant targets. Neurotransmitters act only across the short gap of a synapse, enzymes catalyze reactions, and receptors receive signals rather than carry them.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-080'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'_____ is secreted by adipocytes and suppresses appetite, whereas _____ is secreted by the stomach and stimulates hunger.',choices:['Ghrelin / leptin','Insulin / glucagon','Leptin / ghrelin','Leptin / insulin'],ans:2,exp:'**Leptin** from fat cells tells the hypothalamus that energy stores are adequate, so its level tracks fat mass; mice lacking leptin or its receptor overeat and become obese. **Ghrelin** from the stomach rises before meals to promote hunger, so swapping the two is the classic error.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-080b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'How do steroid hormones typically differ from peptide hormones in their mechanism of action?',choices:['They bind cell-surface receptors and use cAMP','They act faster and their effects are briefer','They are water-soluble and travel unbound in plasma','They bind intracellular receptors and alter transcription'],ans:3,exp:'Lipid-soluble **steroid hormones** diffuse across the membrane, bind cytoplasmic or nuclear receptors and change gene transcription, giving slow but long-lasting effects. Binding surface receptors and signaling through cAMP or IP₃ describes water-soluble **peptide hormones**.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-080c'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'A passage presents two competing hypotheses. When a question asks what the hypotheses have in common, which answer should be selected?',choices:['One that applies to both without adding factors unique to either','One that matches the hypothesis with stronger experimental support','One that includes every factor mentioned in either hypothesis','One that combines the central prediction of each hypothesis'],ans:0,exp:'A shared feature must hold for **both** hypotheses, so eliminate any choice that fits only one or contradicts either. Picking the option that merges every factor from both is tempting, but it adds claims that one of the hypotheses never makes.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-081'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3A',q:'Which statement reflects the settling point model of body weight rather than the set point model?',choices:['The brain defends a fixed, genetically determined weight','Weight can shift with lasting changes in diet and activity','Weight returns to its original value after any diet ends','Hypothalamic signals override environmental influences on weight'],ans:1,exp:'The **settling point** model holds that weight settles wherever genes, metabolism and environment balance, so sustained lifestyle changes can move it. The **set point** model instead proposes that the brain actively defends a fixed weight, explaining why diets supposedly fail long-term.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-082'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'3B',q:'Which organ breaks down its glycogen stores to release glucose into the blood and regulate blood glucose levels?',choices:['Skeletal muscle','Pancreas','Liver','Small intestine'],ans:2,exp:'The **liver** performs glycogenolysis in response to glucagon and epinephrine and can release free glucose because it has **glucose-6-phosphatase**. Skeletal muscle stores glycogen too but lacks that enzyme, so its glucose-6-phosphate stays inside the muscle for its own use.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-083'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which change would NOT interfere with repeated signaling at the neuromuscular junction?',choices:['Blocking acetylcholine release','Blocking nicotinic ACh receptors','Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase','Increasing nicotinic receptor density'],ans:3,exp:'Botulinum toxin blocks **ACh release**, curare blocks **ACh receptors**, and acetylcholinesterase inhibitors let ACh linger, causing persistent depolarization that inactivates the end plate, so all three disrupt signaling. Adding more receptors would, if anything, strengthen the response.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-084'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'At the neuromuscular junction, an action potential arriving at the axon terminal opens _____ channels, which triggers release of _____.',choices:['voltage-gated Ca²⁺ / acetylcholine','voltage-gated Na⁺ / acetylcholine','ligand-gated Ca²⁺ / norepinephrine','voltage-gated Ca²⁺ / norepinephrine'],ans:0,exp:'Depolarization of the terminal opens **voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels**, and Ca²⁺ influx drives vesicle fusion and **acetylcholine** release; ACh then binds nicotinic receptors, Na⁺ influx depolarizes the motor end plate, and acetylcholinesterase ends the signal. Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels propagate the axon\'s action potential but do not trigger exocytosis.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-084b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which muscle types are striated?',choices:['Skeletal and smooth','Skeletal and cardiac','Cardiac and smooth','Skeletal only'],ans:1,exp:'**Skeletal** (voluntary, multinucleate) and **cardiac** (involuntary, with intercalated discs) muscle both have organized sarcomeres that appear as striations. Smooth muscle, found in blood vessels, gut, uterus and bladder, lacks sarcomeres, so pairing it with either striated type is the common trap.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-085'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'A person breathes 10 times per minute with a tidal volume of 800 mL and an anatomical dead space of 150 mL. What is the alveolar ventilation?',choices:['8,000 mL/min','9,500 mL/min','6,500 mL/min','1,500 mL/min'],ans:2,exp:'Alveolar ventilation = (tidal volume − dead space) × rate = (800 − 150 mL) × 10/min = **6,500 mL/min**. The 8,000 mL/min figure is total minute ventilation, which wrongly counts dead-space air that never reaches the alveoli.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-086'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Why is slow, deep breathing more efficient for gas exchange than rapid, shallow breathing at the same minute ventilation?',choices:['Deep breaths reduce the volume of anatomical dead space','Rapid breaths increase hemoglobin affinity for oxygen','Shallow breaths raise the rate of diffusion across alveoli','Fixed dead space is a smaller share of each deep breath'],ans:3,exp:'Each breath must first fill a fixed **anatomical dead space**, so with larger breaths that volume is a smaller fraction and more air reaches the alveoli. Deep breathing does not shrink the dead space itself; rapid shallow breathing mostly moves the same dead-space air back and forth.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-086b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'In the cross-bridge cycle of skeletal muscle, what directly causes the myosin head to detach from actin?',choices:['ATP binding to myosin','ATP hydrolysis by myosin','Ca²⁺ binding to troponin','Release of inorganic phosphate'],ans:0,exp:'**ATP binding** releases the myosin head from actin, and hydrolysis of that ATP then re-cocks the head. Phosphate release accompanies the power stroke, and Ca²⁺ binding to troponin shifts tropomyosin to expose binding sites; the filaments slide past each other without either one shortening.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-087'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which sarcomere region keeps a constant length during skeletal muscle contraction?',choices:['H zone','A band','I band','Z-to-Z distance'],ans:1,exp:'The **A band** equals the length of the thick filaments, which do not shorten, so it stays constant as thin filaments slide past. The H zone and I band, which each contain only one filament type, both shrink, as does the distance between Z lines.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-087b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'What causes rigor mortis after death?',choices:['Excess ATP keeps myosin heads cycling','Ca²⁺ is pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum','Lack of ATP prevents myosin from detaching from actin','Acetylcholine accumulates at the neuromuscular junction'],ans:2,exp:'Myosin needs **ATP binding** to release actin, so when ATP runs out after death the cross-bridges stay locked and muscles remain rigid until the proteins degrade. Ca²⁺ reuptake into the sarcoplasmic reticulum would relax muscle, and that pump itself requires ATP.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-087c'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'The lens fails to form when the optic cup is removed from an embryo. This demonstrates which developmental principle?',choices:['Apoptosis','Determination','Cell migration','Induction'],ans:3,exp:'**Embryonic induction** occurs when one tissue signals a neighboring tissue to change fate; here the optic cup induces the overlying surface ectoderm to form the lens. Determination is a cell\'s commitment to a fate, not the signal from another tissue that brings it about.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-088'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'Which sequence of early embryonic development is in the correct order?',choices:['Cleavage → blastula → gastrulation → neurulation','Cleavage → gastrulation → blastula → neurulation','Blastula → cleavage → neurulation → gastrulation','Cleavage → blastula → neurulation → gastrulation'],ans:0,exp:'After fertilization, **cleavage** yields a morula and then a blastula (blastocyst) that implants; **gastrulation** forms the three germ layers, and **neurulation** then forms the neural tube from ectoderm under induction by the notochord. Neurulation cannot precede gastrulation, because the ectoderm and notochord it depends on arise during gastrulation.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-088b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'What usually causes a tubal (ectopic) pregnancy?',choices:['Fertilization occurring within the uterine cavity','Delayed transport of the embryo through the oviduct','Implantation of the blastocyst in the endometrium','Failure of the corpus luteum to secrete progesterone'],ans:1,exp:'If scarring or impaired ciliary or muscular action slows **transport through the oviduct**, the blastocyst implants in the tube before reaching the uterus. Low progesterone from the corpus luteum leads to loss of the endometrium, not implantation outside the uterus.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-089'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'Fertilization normally occurs in the _____, and implantation occurs about _____ after fertilization.',choices:['uterine cavity / 1–2 days','fallopian tube ampulla / 14 days','fallopian tube ampulla / 6–7 days','uterine cavity / 6–7 days'],ans:2,exp:'Fertilization occurs in the **ampulla** of the fallopian tube; the zygote cleaves as it travels, and the blastocyst embeds in the endometrium about **6–7 days** later. Fourteen days is the typical timing of ovulation within the cycle, not the interval to implantation.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-089b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'What is the main immediate danger of a tubal ectopic pregnancy?',choices:['Excess hCG causing ovarian cysts','Placental invasion of the bladder','Fetal overgrowth in the uterus','Tubal rupture causing hemorrhage'],ans:3,exp:'The fallopian tube cannot expand to hold a growing embryo, so it may **rupture** and cause life-threatening internal **hemorrhage**. Placental invasion into nearby organs is a complication of abnormal uterine implantation, not of a tubal pregnancy.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-090'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which hormonal event directly triggers ovulation?',choices:['A surge of LH from the anterior pituitary','A surge of FSH from the posterior pituitary','A rise in progesterone from the corpus luteum','A fall in estrogen from the developing follicle'],ans:0,exp:'Rising estrogen from the dominant follicle switches to **positive feedback**, prompting the anterior pituitary to release an **LH surge** that triggers ovulation about a day later; a delayed surge delays ovulation. Progesterone rises only after ovulation, once the corpus luteum forms.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-091'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'2C',q:'What do all ectopic pregnancies have in common?',choices:['Fertilization takes place outside the oviduct','Implantation occurs outside the uterine cavity','Ovulation is delayed by a late LH surge','The embryo develops from an unfertilized egg'],ans:1,exp:'By definition an **ectopic pregnancy** is implantation anywhere other than the uterine cavity, most often in the fallopian tube. Ovulation, fertilization and early development usually proceed normally; the error lies in where the blastocyst implants.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-092'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Dilation of skin blood vessels cools the body mainly by increasing heat loss through radiation and conduction. Under which condition would this mechanism fail?',choices:['When the relative humidity is very high','When the person is sweating heavily','When the surroundings are warmer than the skin','When air is moving rapidly over the skin'],ans:2,exp:'Vasodilation brings warm blood to the surface, but **radiation and conduction** move heat only from warmer to cooler objects; if the surroundings are hotter than skin, the body gains heat instead. High humidity impairs **evaporation** of sweat, which is a separate cooling route.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-093'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'When the air temperature is higher than body temperature, which is the only mechanism by which the body can still lose heat?',choices:['Radiation','Conduction','Convection','Evaporation'],ans:3,exp:'Radiation, conduction and convection all transfer heat from warmer to cooler, so in hotter surroundings they add heat to the body. **Evaporation** of sweat and respiratory water removes heat as latent heat of vaporization regardless of air temperature, though high humidity slows it.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-093b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'At an air temperature of 45 °C, which animal\'s body temperature would most closely approach the environmental temperature?',choices:['Lizard','Human','Camel','Kangaroo rat'],ans:0,exp:'A lizard is an **ectotherm** with scaly, relatively impermeable skin and no sweat glands, so it cannot cool by evaporation and its body temperature drifts toward ambient. Humans and camels are endotherms that can sweat, and kangaroo rats are endotherms that avoid daytime heat in cool burrows.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-094'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Compared with endotherms, ectotherms typically have:',choices:['higher metabolic rates and food needs','lower metabolic rates and food needs','constant, internally set body temperatures','greater heat production from brown fat'],ans:1,exp:'**Ectotherms** (reptiles, amphibians, most fish) take their body heat from the environment and regulate temperature through behavior, so their metabolic rates and food and water needs are low. A constant, internally generated body temperature is the hallmark of **endotherms** such as mammals and birds.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-094b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Why can severe dehydration lead to acute kidney failure?',choices:['Plasma becomes too dilute for filtration','ADH secretion stops, causing water loss','Low blood pressure drops the glomerular filtration rate','The loop of Henle reabsorbs too much sodium'],ans:2,exp:'Severe fluid loss lowers blood volume and pressure, so glomerular hydrostatic pressure falls and the **GFR** cannot be maintained. ADH secretion actually rises during dehydration, and plasma becomes more concentrated, not more dilute.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-095'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Desert mammals such as kangaroo rats produce very concentrated urine mainly because they have:',choices:['low levels of circulating ADH','short proximal convoluted tubules','fewer aquaporins in collecting ducts','exceptionally long loops of Henle'],ans:3,exp:'Long **loops of Henle** act as a countercurrent multiplier that builds a steep medullary salt gradient, so collecting ducts under **ADH** can draw out more water. Low ADH or fewer aquaporins would make the urine more dilute, not more concentrated.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-095b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Why are people who lack sweat glands especially prone to heat stroke in hot tropical climates?',choices:['Evaporation is the only cooling route left in such heat','Vasodilation is impaired whenever sweat glands are absent','Their basal metabolic rate rises sharply in hot weather','Their skin absorbs more radiation without sweat glands'],ans:0,exp:'When air temperature nears or exceeds body temperature, radiation and conduction stop removing heat or even add it, so **evaporation** of sweat becomes the only effective cooling mechanism. Vasodilation still occurs in these people but cannot shed heat to warm surroundings.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-096'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Superantigens, such as the toxin in toxic shock syndrome, cause harm mainly by:',choices:['inhibiting antigen processing in dendritic cells','nonspecifically activating a large fraction of T cells','destroying CD4⁺ helper T cells by direct lysis','blocking antibody binding to bacterial surfaces'],ans:1,exp:'Superantigens bind **MHC II** and the T-cell receptor outside the normal peptide-binding site, bypassing antigen processing and activating a huge fraction of T cells at once. The resulting **cytokine storm** causes fever, hypotension and shock; they skip processing by APCs rather than inhibiting it.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-097'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'An antigen-presenting cell displays processed peptide on _____ for recognition by _____ T cells.',choices:['MHC I / CD4⁺ helper','MHC I / CD8⁺ cytotoxic','MHC II / CD4⁺ helper','MHC II / CD8⁺ cytotoxic'],ans:2,exp:'APCs (dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells) take up exogenous antigen and present peptide on **MHC II** to **CD4⁺ helper T cells**, which also require co-stimulation. MHC I instead presents endogenous peptides to CD8⁺ cytotoxic T cells (mnemonic: 2 × 4 = 8 and 1 × 8 = 8).',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-097b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'In which lymphoid organ do T cells mature?',choices:['Spleen','Lymph nodes','Bone marrow','Thymus'],ans:3,exp:'T-cell precursors arise in bone marrow but migrate to the **thymus** to mature (T for thymus), whereas B cells mature in the **bone marrow**. The spleen and lymph nodes are secondary lymphoid organs where mature lymphocytes encounter antigen.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-098'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'A superantigen activates 20% of a person\'s T cells, while a typical antigen activates about 1 in 100,000. By what factor does the superantigen exceed the typical antigen?',choices:['20,000-fold','2,000-fold','200,000-fold','5,000-fold'],ans:0,exp:'Convert both to fractions and divide: 0.20 ÷ (1/100,000) = 0.20 × 100,000 = **20,000-fold**. The other values reflect power-of-ten slips, so always write percentages as decimals before dividing.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-099'},
  {s:'Passage & Data Skills',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Strain A requires 10⁵ bacteria to cause infection, and strain B requires 5 × 10⁴. How do their potencies compare?',choices:['A is twice as potent as B','B is twice as potent as A','B is five times as potent as A','A and B are equally potent'],ans:1,exp:'Potency is **inversely proportional** to the dose needed: B needs half as many bacteria (5 × 10⁴ vs 10⁵), so B is **twice** as potent. Treating the larger required dose as more potent reverses the relationship.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-100'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'After gallbladder removal, which dietary component should be limited, and why?',choices:['Proteins, because pepsin secretion falls','Starches, because amylase release is delayed','Fats, because bile is not stored for large meals','Fiber, because bile is needed to digest cellulose'],ans:2,exp:'The gallbladder **stores and concentrates bile**; without it, bile trickles continuously from the liver, leaving no large dose to **emulsify** a fatty meal. Protein and starch digestion rely on gastric and pancreatic enzymes, not bile.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-101'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'After absorption into enterocytes, dietary fats are reassembled into triglycerides and enter the circulation as:',choices:['micelles absorbed directly into capillaries','free fatty acids bound to albumin in portal blood','LDL particles secreted into the hepatic portal vein','chylomicrons that enter lacteals of the lymphatic system'],ans:3,exp:'Bile salts emulsify fat, **pancreatic lipase** releases fatty acids and monoglycerides, and micelles deliver them to enterocytes, which repackage triglycerides into **chylomicrons**. Chylomicrons are too large for capillaries, so they enter **lacteals** and reach the blood via the thoracic duct rather than the portal vein.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-101b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which hormone, released by the duodenum in response to fats and proteins, stimulates gallbladder contraction?',choices:['Cholecystokinin','Secretin','Gastrin','Ghrelin'],ans:0,exp:'**Cholecystokinin (CCK)** from duodenal I cells contracts the gallbladder and stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion. **Secretin** responds to acid entering the duodenum and mainly triggers bicarbonate release from the pancreas.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-101c'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'Acetylation of lysine residues on histones affects gene expression by:',choices:['adding positive charge and tightening chromatin','neutralizing positive charge and loosening chromatin','adding negative charge to DNA and blocking transcription','neutralizing negative charge on DNA and silencing genes'],ans:1,exp:'Histones (an octamer of H2A, H2B, H3 and H4, with H1 as the linker histone) are rich in **positively charged** lysine and arginine that bind negatively charged DNA. **Acetylation** neutralizes lysine\'s charge, loosening chromatin and promoting transcription, while deacetylation tightens it.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-102'},
  {s:'Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression',disc:'biology',aamc:'1B',q:'Compared with euchromatin, heterochromatin is:',choices:['loosely packed and transcriptionally active','loosely packed and mostly silent','tightly packed and mostly silent','tightly packed and transcriptionally active'],ans:2,exp:'**Heterochromatin** is tightly condensed, stains darkly and is largely transcriptionally silent, as at centromeres and telomeres. **Euchromatin** is loosely packed, stains lightly and contains most actively transcribed genes.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-102b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which structure visible on an X-ray indicates that a child\'s long bones are still growing?',choices:['Haversian canals','Compact cortical bone','Red bone marrow','Epiphyseal plates'],ans:3,exp:'Cartilaginous **epiphyseal (growth) plates** near the ends of long bones appear as gaps on X-ray and allow lengthening until they ossify after puberty. Haversian canals are microscopic features of compact bone that persist in adults.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-103'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Flat bones of the skull form by _____ ossification, whereas long bones form by _____ ossification.',choices:['intramembranous / endochondral','endochondral / intramembranous','endochondral / appositional','intramembranous / appositional'],ans:0,exp:'In **intramembranous ossification**, bone forms directly from mesenchyme, as in flat skull bones and the clavicle. In **endochondral ossification**, a cartilage model is replaced by bone, as in long bones that keep lengthening at their epiphyseal plates.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-103b'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'The structural unit of compact bone, made of concentric lamellae around a central canal that carries vessels and nerves, is the:',choices:['trabecula','osteon','lacuna','canaliculus'],ans:1,exp:'An **osteon** (Haversian system) has a central canal surrounded by rings of lamellae, with osteocytes housed in lacunae and linked by canaliculi. **Trabeculae** are the struts of spongy bone, which holds red marrow, not units of compact bone.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-103c'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Although the keratinized epidermis is largely waterproof, sweat still reaches the skin surface because:',choices:['sweat diffuses across the stratum corneum','sweat glands lie within the epidermis itself','ducts carry sweat from dermal glands to pores','keratinocytes actively transport sweat outward'],ans:2,exp:'Sweat glands sit in the **dermis** and empty through **ducts** that pass through the epidermis to surface pores, so sweat never has to cross the keratinized layer. Diffusion across the stratum corneum is exactly what its keratin and lipids prevent.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-105'},
  {s:'Organ Systems & Physiology',disc:'biology',aamc:'3B',q:'Which feature distinguishes eccrine sweat glands from apocrine sweat glands?',choices:['They open into hair follicles','They are concentrated in the armpits and groin','They become active only after puberty','They secrete watery sweat used for cooling'],ans:3,exp:'**Eccrine** glands are spread over the body, open directly onto the skin and secrete watery sweat for thermoregulation under sympathetic but **cholinergic** control. **Apocrine** glands in the armpits and groin open into hair follicles, release a protein- and lipid-rich secretion and become active at puberty.',src:'aamc-bio-qpack-1:BIO-105b'},
];
